:"Vietnam: Rafael Spike (2026)","y":250000000},{"x":"Colombia: coal exports halted per year (est.)","y":200000000},{"x":"India: arms trade with Israel (2024)","y":185000000},{"x":"Morocco: trade with Israel (2023)","y":116700000},{"x":"South Korea: arms exports to Israel, Oct 2023–Apr 2024 (min.)","y":1285000}],"src":"Colombia One, Newstapa, Al Jazeera, Calcalist (CTech), Military Africa, Observer Research Foundation, Times of Israel"},{"id":"SER-X005","case":"12","label":"Turkey's exports to Israel before and after the trade ban","unit":"USD","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"Sept 2023","y":460300000},{"x":"Sept 2024","y":1200}],"src":"Turkish Minute"},{"id":"SER-X006","case":"12","label":"Share of Israel's coal imports, 2023–24 (UN Special Rapporteur)","unit":"%","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"Colombia","y":60},{"x":"South Africa (Glencore shipments)","y":15}],"src":"London Mining Network (quoting UN Special Rapporteur report A/HRC/59/23)"},{"id":"SER-X007","case":"SL","label":"People killed in Lebanon by Israeli attacks since 8 Oct 2023 (Lebanese Ministry of Public Health, cumulative)","unit":"people","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"4 Oct 2024","y":1900},{"x":"4 Dec 2024","y":4047},{"x":"17 Sep 2026","y":8953}],"src":"ABC News, Arab News (citing Lebanese Health Ministry), Palestinian Information Center (citing Lebanese Health Ministry)"},{"id":"SER-X008","case":"SL","label":"People displaced in Lebanon: peaks and those still displaced","unit":"people","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"Oct 2024 (Lebanese gov.)","y":1200000},{"x":"25 Nov 2024 (IOM DTM)","y":899725},{"x":"Nov 2025 still displaced (UN)","y":64000},{"x":"16 Mar 2026 self-registered (WHO)","y":1049328},{"x":"End Jul 2026 still displaced (UN CERD)","y":360000},{"x":"Sep 2026 still displaced (UN via TNH)","y":341000}],"src":"ABC News, UN OCHA, Times of Israel (citing UN), WHO EMRO, UN CERD, The New Humanitarian"},{"id":"SER-X009","case":"SL","label":"South Lebanon villages destroyed or flattened, by source","unit":"villages","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"Completely destroyed, Nov 2024 (Information International)","y":29},{"x":"Devastated, Nov 2024 (National News Agency)","y":37},{"x":"Heavily damaged or flattened inside Yellow Line, May 2026 (Bellingcat, of 54)","y":46},{"x":"Completely razed, admitted by Israel Katz, Jul 2026","y":24},{"x":"Occupied by Israel, Sep 2026 (The New Humanitarian)","y":68}],"src":"L'Orient Today, Bellingcat, Times of Israel, The New Humanitarian"},{"id":"SER-X010","case":"SL","label":"Cost of Israel's assaults on Lebanon","unit":"USD","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"2024 damage + losses (World Bank RDNA)","y":14000000000},{"x":"2024 reconstruction needs (World Bank RDNA)","y":11000000000},{"x":"2026 direct damage, upper estimate (Lebanese gov.)","y":4000000000},{"x":"Total since 2023, upper estimate (PM Salam)","y":27000000000}],"src":"World Bank, Xinhua (quoting Lebanese Information Minister Paul Morcos), Middle East Monitor (quoting PM Nawaf Salam)"},{"id":"SER-X011","case":"PER-N-TRUMP","label":"Arms deals approved for Israel under Trump, by date (Jan 2025 to Sep 2026; reported total $21.6bn)","unit":"USD","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"2,000-lb bombs (early 2025)","y":2040000000},{"x":"Feb 2025","y":7400000000},{"x":"Jun 2025","y":510000000},{"x":"Jan 2026","y":6670000000},{"x":"Mar 2026 (emergency)","y":650000000},{"x":"May 2026 (emergency)","y":992400000},{"x":"Sep 2026","y":2800000000}],"src":"The New Arab, The Times of Israel"},{"id":"SER-X012","case":"PER-N-TRUMP","label":"Money tied to Trump, his family and his businesses from pro-Israel donors and regional states (reported values)","unit":"USD","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"Adelson to pro-Trump super PAC (2024)","y":100000000},{"x":"Saudi PIF into Kushner's Affinity","y":2000000000},{"x":"Saudi govt fees to Affinity (2021-24)","y":87000000},{"x":"Qatari 747-8 jet","y":400000000},{"x":"Qatar Trump golf resort project","y":5500000000},{"x":"Saudi Trump-branded project (Dar Global)","y":1000000000}],"src":"Jewish Telegraphic Agency, House Oversight Democrats / Senate Finance Democrats, CBS News, Newsweek, PBS NewsHour"},{"id":"SER-X013","case":"PER-00022","label":"Palantir's contracts with ICE (award value)","unit":"USD","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"All ICE contracts, 2011 to June 2026","y":435000000},{"x":"ICM case management, 2022 (5 yrs)","y":95900000},{"x":"ICM renewal, 2019 (3 yrs)","y":49874018},{"x":"ImmigrationOS, April 2025","y":30000000}],"src":"AFSC Investigate, ExecutiveBiz, GovTech, Immigration Policy Tracking Project (citing Wired)"},{"id":"SER-X014","case":"PER-00022","label":"Palantir's war and deportation contracts (ceiling or award value)","unit":"USD","kind":"bar","points":[{"x":"US Army enterprise deal, 2025 (up to, 10 yrs)","y":10000000000},{"x":"Maven Smart System (ceiling to 2029)","y":1300000000},{"x":"ICE contracts, 2011 to June 2026","y":435000000},{"x":"ImmigrationOS for ICE, 2025","y":30000000}],"src":"Breaking Defense, Wikipedia, AFSC Investigate, Immigration Policy Tracking Project (citing Wired)"}],"reports":{"case10":{"title":"The AI kill chain","dek":"How Unit 8200's mass surveillance of Palestinians ran on U.S. clouds, was turned into kill lists by AI targeting systems, and became revenue for Palantir, Microsoft, Amazon, Google, OpenAI, Oracle and Anthropic, under a Trump administration their founders helped fund.","sections":[{"h":"From phone call to airstrike","body":["The UN Commission of Inquiry concluded on 16 September 2025 that Israeli authorities and forces committed four of the five genocidal acts defined in the 1948 Genocide Convention in Gaza. UN Special Rapporteur Francesca Albanese's July 2025 report, 'From economy of occupation to economy of genocide', traced the corporate machinery behind it and found that Microsoft, Alphabet and Amazon 'grant Israel virtually government-wide access to their cloud and artificial intelligence technologies, enhancing data processing, decision-making and surveillance.'","Investigations by +972 Magazine, Local Call, The Guardian and AP since 2023 let us follow that machinery step by step: Palestinians' calls and messages are intercepted by Unit 8200, stored on U.S. clouds, transcribed and translated with U.S. AI models, scored by Israeli targeting systems such as Lavender and the Gospel, and turned into strikes on homes. In 2026 the same model, with Palantir's Maven Smart System at its core, was used by the U.S. military in the war on Iran, where a strike on a school in Minab killed more than 150 people, most of them children.","This case file sets out each link in that chain, the companies supplying it, what they earned, and the political ties that protect them. Every claim below is attributed to a named source; where only media have reported a link, we say so."],"src":["SRC-V0025","SRC-V0004","SRC-V0079","SRC-W0004","SRC-V0127","SRC-W0003"]},{"h":"Collect and store: Unit 8200 on Microsoft Azure","body":["A joint investigation by The Guardian, +972 Magazine and Local Call (August 2025) found that Israel's military intelligence Unit 8200 used a segregated area of Microsoft's Azure cloud to store intercepted Palestinian phone calls, with an internal aim of 'a million calls an hour.' By the time of +972's September 2025 follow-up, some 11,500 terabytes of Israeli military data, about 195 million hours of audio, sat in Microsoft's data center in the Netherlands.","Unit 8200 sources told the reporters that this data 'has been used over the past two years to plan lethal airstrikes in Gaza, as well as to arrest Palestinians in the West Bank.' The arrangement followed a November 2021 meeting in Seattle between Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella and the unit's then commander, Yossi Sariel.","Microsoft first said in May 2025 that it had found no evidence its technology was used to harm people. Four months later its vice chair Brad Smith wrote that Microsoft had 'found evidence that supports elements of The Guardian's reporting', including Israel Ministry of Defense consumption of Azure storage in the Netherlands and use of AI services, and that it would 'cease and disable specified IMOD subscriptions.' Al Jazeera noted that the vast majority of Microsoft's contract with the Israeli military remained in place, and +972's sources said the unit planned to move the data to Amazon Web Services instead."],"src":["SRC-W0016","SRC-V0011","SRC-W0009","SRC-0046","SRC-V0274","SRC-V0128"]},{"h":"Nimbus: Google and Amazon under contract to the occupation","body":["Project Nimbus is the Israeli government's cloud contract, worth about $1.2B (NIS 4B). Google Cloud and Amazon Web Services won it in 2021; Microsoft was not part of it. Albanese's UN report says the deal is largely funded through Ministry of Defense expenditure and supplies 'core tech infrastructure' to the state.","Google knew the risks before it signed. The Intercept reported in May 2025 that a confidential internal assessment from January 2021 warned that 'Google Cloud Services could be used for, or linked to, the facilitation of human rights violations, including Israeli activity in the West Bank', that Google would get only 'very limited visibility' into use, and that it was 'not permitted to restrict the types of services and information that the Government (including the Ministry of Defense and Israeli Security Agency) chooses to migrate.'","Leaked Nimbus terms reported by The Guardian, +972 and Local Call in October 2025 bar Google and Amazon from suspending service even if Israel breaks their policies, and set up a coded 'winking mechanism': small payments in shekels matching a country's dialing code to tip Israel off when a foreign court or government demands its data. Google fired some 50 workers who protested the contract in April 2024, The Register reported.","Amazon's role reaches into military intelligence directly. +972 and Local Call reported in August 2024 that AWS gives Israel's Military Intelligence Directorate a server farm 'to store masses of intelligence information', which one source called 'the endless storage', and that it supplied 'supplementary information' ahead of airstrikes, some of which killed civilians. Who Profits names AWS as a main cloud provider to the Israel Ministry of Defense and the military, and to the state weapons makers Rafael and Israel Aerospace Industries."],"src":["SRC-V0118","SRC-V0004","SRC-W0017","SRC-W0018","SRC-V0120","SRC-W0019","SRC-V0119","SRC-W0020"]},{"h":"Process: U.S. AI models inside the Israeli military","body":["An AP investigation in February 2025 found that the Israeli military's use of Microsoft and OpenAI artificial intelligence spiked in March 2024 to nearly 200 times its level in the week before 7 October 2023, and that the data it stored on Microsoft servers doubled to more than 13.6 petabytes by July 2024. The military used Azure to transcribe and translate phone calls, texts and audio messages gathered through mass surveillance. OpenAI's models reached it through Microsoft's Azure platform.","+972 Magazine and Local Call reported in January 2025 that between October 2023 and June 2024 the Israel Ministry of Defense spent $10M on 19,000 hours of Microsoft engineering support. Users included Unit 8200, Unit 81, the Air Force's Ofek unit, which manages the 'target bank' for airstrikes, and 'Rolling Stone', the system that tracks the Palestinian population registry and movement.","Google was in the same race. The Washington Post reported in January 2025, citing internal documents, that Google staff gave Israel's Ministry of Defense expanded access to its Vertex AI platform in the weeks after 7 October 2023, partly for fear the work would go to Amazon, and that the military requested Gemini to build an AI assistant for processing documents and audio.","In March 2025 the same reporting team revealed that Unit 8200 was building its own ChatGPT-like model trained on '100 billion words of Arabic obtained in part through Israel's large-scale surveillance of Palestinians.' One intelligence source said: 'AI amplifies power. It allows operations [utilizing] the data of far more people, enabling population control.'"],"src":["SRC-W0021","SRC-V0127","SRC-W0008","SRC-W0022","SRC-V0125","SRC-W0013"]},{"h":"Target: Lavender, the Gospel and 'Where's Daddy?'","body":["+972 Magazine and Local Call revealed in November 2023 that the Israeli military used an AI system called Habsora ('the Gospel') that can 'generate' targets almost automatically, which a former intelligence officer described as a 'mass assassination factory'. Former chief of staff Aviv Kochavi was quoted: 'in the past there were times in Gaza when we would create 50 targets per year. And here the machine produced 100 targets in one day.'","In April 2024 the same outlets reported that a second system, Lavender, marked some 37,000 Palestinians as suspected militants. Officers said they spent about '20 seconds' per target and acted as a 'rubber stamp', although the system made errors in about 10 percent of cases. For junior suspects, the army permitted killing 'up to 15 or 20 civilians'; for a senior commander, 'more than 100'.","A third tool, 'Where's Daddy?', tracked marked people and flagged when they entered their family homes, where they were bombed, often with unguided 'dumb' bombs. 'It's much easier to bomb a family's home,' one source said. 'The system is built to look for them in these situations.' Albanese's UN report names Lavender, the Gospel and Where's Daddy? among the AI systems Israel uses to process targets."],"src":["SRC-W0005","SRC-W0004","SRC-V0004"]},{"h":"Palantir: the targeting company","body":["Palantir opened its first Israeli office in 2015. In January 2024 co-founders Alex Karp and Peter Thiel held the company's board meeting in Tel Aviv 'in solidarity with Israel' and agreed a strategic partnership with the Israel Ministry of Defense to 'harness Palantir's advanced technology in support of war-related missions.'","Al Jazeera reported in 2026 that Palantir has actively recruited veterans of Unit 8200 and that its software 'integrates intercepted communications, satellite imagery and other datasets to help generate military targeting lists for Israeli forces.' Albanese's UN report found 'reasonable grounds to believe' Palantir provided automatic predictive policing technology and its Artificial Intelligence Platform, 'which allows real-time battlefield data integration for automated decision-making'. Confronted in April 2025 with the accusation that Palantir had killed Palestinians, Karp answered: 'mostly terrorists, that's true.'","Karp called himself 'the most publicly supportive CEO of Israel' in July 2026 and said: 'My position is they are a very important partner, and we are proud to support them.'"],"src":["SRC-W0015","SRC-W0014","SRC-0042","SRC-V0004","SRC-W0023"]},{"h":"Maven, Claude and the Minab school","body":["The kill-chain model tested on Gaza became U.S. doctrine in the war on Iran. Palantir's Maven Smart System, the Pentagon's main AI targeting platform, started under a $480M contract in May 2024; the ceiling was raised to nearly $1.3B through 2029 in May 2025. Reuters reported in March 2026 that Deputy Secretary of Defense Steve Feinberg had ordered Maven made an official program of record and that it was already 'the primary AI operating system for the U.S. military, which has carried out thousands of targeted strikes against Iran.'","Claude was embedded in Palantir's Maven Smart System during the 2026 Iran campaign and was reported by the Washington Post to support target generation and prioritization. Bloomberg's investigation found that Maven played a specific role in the Minab targeting chain and that overreliance on the system contributed to the strike. No public evidence currently establishes that Claude itself selected, recommended or processed the Minab school target.","According to the Washington Post, 'Maven, powered by Claude, suggested hundreds of targets, issued precise location coordinates, and prioritized those targets according to importance' during planning for the strikes. Reuters reported that Maven 'uses multiple prompts and workflows that were built using Anthropic's Claude code.' The Guardian argued on 26 March 2026 that Maven's target-identification functions are older computer-vision and sensor-fusion systems, and that large language models such as Claude were added late in 2024 as a natural-language layer over them rather than as the part choosing what to hit.","The strike on the Shajareh Tayyebeh elementary school in Minab on 28 February 2026, the first day of the U.S.–Israeli war on Iran, killed more than 150 people, including at least 123 children, according to Bloomberg's count. Bloomberg found that the site, catalogued as a Revolutionary Guard facility on outdated data, was fed into Maven and came out as a recommended day-one target, although satellite images from 2018 showed a school's painted walls, playground and soccer pitch. Palantir told Bloomberg it 'is not responsible for the underlying data nor identifying intelligence deficiencies.'","Anthropic's CEO Dario Amodei said in June 2026: 'we don't have access to, we don't know exactly how these models were used.' Days before the war, the Trump administration had ordered agencies to stop using Anthropic after it refused to drop two limits, on fully autonomous weapons and mass domestic surveillance; OpenAI announced its own classified Pentagon deal within hours."],"src":["SRC-W0001","SRC-W0002","SRC-V0309","SRC-W0006","SRC-W0007","SRC-W0024","SRC-W0012","SRC-V0348","SRC-W0003","SRC-V0007","SRC-V0349","SRC-V0134","SRC-V0133"]},{"h":"Who profits","body":["Palantir's revenue has doubled since the Gaza genocide began: from $2.2B in 2023 to $4.5B in 2025, and $1.94B in Q2 2026 alone, up 93% year on year, with U.S. government revenue up 90% to $809M. It raised its 2026 forecast to about $8.15B. Its contracts include a U.S. Army enterprise deal worth up to $10B and Maven at nearly $1.3B.","The cloud and AI giants grew alongside. Google Cloud revenue reached $24.77B in Q2 2026, up 82%; AWS segment sales were $42.2B, up 37%; Microsoft earned $133.7B on $331.8B revenue in fiscal 2026 and owns 27% of OpenAI, worth about $135B. Amazon booked a $53.4B gain in Q2 2026, mainly from its Anthropic stake, and Microsoft a $3.2B gain on the same company. Oracle's contracted backlog rose from $455B in September 2025 to $664B by the quarter reported in September 2026.","Markets priced the war in. Reuters calculated in October 2025 that weapons makers' stocks were more than 120% higher than on 7 October 2023, and MSCI's Israel index more than 80% higher. The UN Special Rapporteur names BlackRock and Vanguard as top institutional investors in Palantir, Microsoft, Amazon and Alphabet."],"src":["SRC-V0337","SRC-W0015","SRC-V0116","SRC-W0001","SRC-V0126","SRC-V0121","SRC-V0122","SRC-V0129","SRC-V0130","SRC-W0025","SRC-W0011","SRC-W0026","SRC-W0027","SRC-V0004"]},{"h":"The politics: Karp, Thiel, Ellison and the Trump administration","body":["Peter Thiel wrote in 2009: 'I no longer believe that freedom and democracy are compatible.' He is the political patron of Vice President JD Vance, who worked for his venture fund Mithril Capital, not for Palantir. Multinationals Observatory documented in June 2026 how former Palantir staff and Thiel associates now hold posts across the Trump administration, including the federal chief information officer. Karp gave $1M to the pro-Trump super PAC MAGA Inc in December 2024.","Larry Ellison gave about $45M to a nonprofit backing Trump's 2024 campaign, Fortune reported; on 21 January 2025 he stood beside Trump to launch Stargate, the $500B AI infrastructure venture in which Oracle is an initial equity funder. Oracle took 15% of TikTok's new U.S. joint venture in 2026, the administration cleared the Skydance–Paramount merger, and Warner Bros. Discovery shareholders approved a $110B merger with Paramount Skydance in April 2026. Ellison has given more than $26M to Friends of the Israel Defense Forces, Responsible Statecraft reported, including $16.6M in 2017, then the largest single gift in its history.","Sam Altman gave $1M to Trump's inaugural committee and said at the Stargate launch: 'I think if it were a different President, it might not have been possible.' OpenAI president Greg Brockman and his wife gave $25M to MAGA Inc. On 29 September 2026 Trump gathered the heads of Google, Microsoft, OpenAI, Anthropic, Meta, Nvidia and others to sign a voluntary AI accord he called 'almost like a constitution', enforced by what he called 'self-policing'. No binding rule stops any of these firms from selling targeting infrastructure to an army accused of genocide."],"src":["SRC-W0028","SRC-W0029","SRC-V0022","SRC-W0030","SRC-W0010","SRC-V0023","SRC-V0012","SRC-V0013","SRC-W0031","SRC-0048","SRC-V0233","SRC-W0032","SRC-W0033"]}],"quotes":[{"text":"We were not interested in killing [Hamas] operatives only when they were in a military building or engaged in a military activity.","who":"A., Israeli intelligence officer, to +972 Magazine and Local Call (April 2024)","src":"SRC-W0004"},{"text":"in the past there were times in Gaza when we would create 50 targets per year. And here the machine produced 100 targets in one day.","who":"Aviv Kochavi, former Israeli military chief of staff, quoted by +972 Magazine","src":"SRC-W0005"},{"text":"AI amplifies power. It allows operations [utilizing] the data of far more people, enabling population control.","who":"Israeli intelligence source on Unit 8200's language model, to +972 Magazine, Local Call and The Guardian (March 2025)","src":"SRC-W0013"},{"text":"The crazy wealth of services, big data and AI — we've already reached a point where our systems really need it","who":"Col. Racheli Dembinsky, commander of the Israeli military's computing center, July 2024 lecture reported by +972","src":"SRC-V0119"},{"text":"The implications are enormous for the role of tech in enabling this type of unethical and unlawful warfare going forward","who":"Heidy Khlaaf, chief AI scientist at the AI Now Institute, to AP","src":"SRC-V0127"},{"text":"My position is they are a very important partner, and we are proud to support them","who":"Alex Karp, Palantir CEO, on Israel (July 2026)","src":"SRC-W0023"}],"chain":[{"step":"Collect","what":"Mass interception of Palestinian calls, messages and movement; population registry ('Rolling Stone')","systems":["Unit 8200 intercepts","Rolling Stone registry"],"who":["Unit 8200","Israel Defense Forces (IDF)"],"src":["SRC-W0009","SRC-W0008","SRC-W0013"]},{"step":"Store","what":"Cloud storage for intercepts and intelligence: 11,500 TB on Azure in the Netherlands; AWS server farm for military intelligence; Nimbus","systems":["Microsoft Azure","AWS","Google Cloud","Project Nimbus"],"who":["Microsoft","Amazon Web Services (AWS)","Google Cloud","Project Nimbus"],"src":["SRC-W0009","SRC-V0119","SRC-V0118","SRC-V0004"]},{"step":"Process","what":"Transcription, translation and search with commercial AI; Unit 8200's own LLM trained on intercepted Arabic","systems":["Azure AI","OpenAI GPT-4 via Azure","Vertex AI","Gemini (requested)"],"who":["Microsoft","OpenAI","Google Cloud","Unit 8200"],"src":["SRC-V0127","SRC-W0008","SRC-V0125","SRC-W0013"]},{"step":"Target","what":"AI systems score people and buildings and flag when targets are home; Palantir software helps build targeting lists; Maven ranks targets for Iran","systems":["Lavender","The Gospel","Where's Daddy?","Maven Smart System","Claude (in Maven)"],"who":["Israel Defense Forces (IDF)","Palantir Technologies","Anthropic","U.S. Department of Defense"],"src":["SRC-W0004","SRC-W0005","SRC-W0015","SRC-V0309","SRC-W0007"]},{"step":"Strike","what":"Air strikes on family homes in Gaza; Tomahawk strike on the Minab school in Iran","systems":["Ofek target bank","unguided bombs","Tomahawk"],"who":["Israel Defense Forces (IDF)","U.S. Department of Defense"],"src":["SRC-W0004","SRC-W0008","SRC-W0003","SRC-V0007"]},{"step":"Profit","what":"Cloud and AI revenue, Pentagon contracts, stock gains for founders and asset managers","systems":["Nimbus $1.2B","Maven ~$1.3B","Army up to $10B"],"who":["Palantir Technologies","Microsoft","Amazon","Alphabet","Oracle","BlackRock","Vanguard"],"src":["SRC-V0337","SRC-W0015","SRC-W0001","SRC-V0116","SRC-V0004","SRC-W0026"]},{"step":"Protect","what":"Donations, Stargate and a voluntary AI accord keep the sector unregulated","systems":["Stargate $500B","MAGA Inc","Sept 2026 AI accord"],"who":["Donald Trump","Alex Karp","Peter Thiel","Larry Ellison","Sam Altman"],"src":["SRC-V0022","SRC-W0030","SRC-W0010","SRC-W0032"]}]},"COM-00010":{"title":"Palantir: the targeting company","dek":"Palantir turned the Gaza genocide and the Iran war into its fastest growth ever, while its founders funded the politicians who buy its software.","sections":[{"h":"Partner in 'war-related missions'","body":["Palantir opened its first Israeli office in 2015. In January 2024 Alex Karp and Peter Thiel held its board meeting in Tel Aviv 'in solidarity with Israel' and agreed a strategic partnership with the Israel Ministry of Defense to 'harness Palantir's advanced technology in support of war-related missions.'","Al Jazeera reported that Palantir actively recruited Unit 8200 veterans and that its software 'integrates intercepted communications, satellite imagery and other datasets to help generate military targeting lists for Israeli forces.' UN Special Rapporteur Francesca Albanese found 'reasonable grounds to believe' it provided predictive policing technology and its AI Platform for 'automated decision-making' on the battlefield."],"src":["SRC-0042","SRC-W0015","SRC-W0014","SRC-V0004"]},{"h":"Maven and the Minab school","body":["Palantir's Maven Smart System started as a $480M Pentagon contract in 2024; the ceiling rose to nearly $1.3B in 2025 and in March 2026 the Pentagon made it a program of record, already 'the primary AI operating system' for U.S. strikes on Iran, Reuters reported.","Claude was embedded in Palantir's Maven Smart System during the 2026 Iran campaign and was reported by the Washington Post to support target generation and prioritization. Bloomberg's investigation found that Maven played a specific role in the Minab targeting chain and that overreliance on the system contributed to the strike. No public evidence currently establishes that Claude itself selected, recommended or processed the Minab school target.","Bloomberg found the Minab school, wrongly catalogued as a Revolutionary Guard site, was fed into Maven and came out as a recommended day-one target; the strike killed more than 150 people, at least 123 of them children. Palantir said it 'is not responsible for the underlying data nor identifying intelligence deficiencies.'"],"src":["SRC-W0001","SRC-W0002","SRC-V0309","SRC-V0348","SRC-W0003"]},{"h":"The money","body":["Revenue rose from $2.2B in 2023 to $4.5B in 2025. In Q2 2026 Palantir reported $1.94B, up 93%, with U.S. government revenue up 90% to $809M, and raised its 2026 forecast to about $8.15B. H1 2026 net income was $1.94B. Contracts include a U.S. Army deal worth up to $10B, ICE's $30M 'ImmigrationOS', and a £240M ($323M) UK Ministry of Defence contract in January 2026. Vanguard (9.1%) and BlackRock (8.6%) are its largest institutional investors, per the UN report."],"src":["SRC-V0337","SRC-W0015","SRC-V0336","SRC-V0116","SRC-V0004"]}]},"COM-00016":{"title":"Microsoft: the cloud behind Unit 8200","dek":"Microsoft's Azure stored Israel's mass surveillance of Palestinians and carried OpenAI models to the Israeli military.","sections":[{"h":"A million calls an hour","body":["The Guardian, +972 and Local Call revealed in August 2025 that Unit 8200 stored intercepted Palestinian calls on Azure, aiming for 'a million calls an hour', after Yossi Sariel met CEO Satya Nadella in Seattle in November 2021. About 11,500 terabytes sat in Microsoft's Netherlands data center. Sources said the data was used 'to plan lethal airstrikes in Gaza, as well as to arrest Palestinians in the West Bank.'"],"src":["SRC-W0016","SRC-W0009","SRC-V0011"]},{"h":"AI for the army","body":["AP found the Israeli military's use of Microsoft and OpenAI AI spiked to nearly 200 times pre-7 October levels by March 2024, with stored data doubling to more than 13.6 petabytes. +972 reported the Israel Ministry of Defense bought 19,000 hours of Microsoft engineering support for $10M between October 2023 and June 2024, serving units including Unit 8200 and the Air Force's Ofek target-bank unit. Microsoft itself confirms it provides the ministry with Azure cloud and AI services, including translation."],"src":["SRC-V0127","SRC-W0008","SRC-0046"]},{"h":"A partial cut-off, full profits","body":["In September 2025 Brad Smith said Microsoft had 'found evidence that supports elements of The Guardian's reporting' and disabled 'specified IMOD subscriptions'. The vast majority of its Israeli military contract stayed in place, Al Jazeera reported, and the unit planned to move the data to AWS. Microsoft earned $133.7B on $331.8B revenue in fiscal 2026 and owns 27% of OpenAI, worth about $135B."],"src":["SRC-V0274","SRC-V0128","SRC-W0009","SRC-V0129","SRC-V0130"]}]},"COM-00011":{"title":"Amazon and AWS: endless storage","dek":"Amazon's cloud holds Israeli military intelligence and half of Project Nimbus, and its Anthropic stake made it billions in 2026.","sections":[{"h":"Nimbus and the intelligence server farm","body":["AWS and Google won Project Nimbus, Israel's $1.2B government cloud contract, in 2021. +972 and Local Call reported in August 2024 that AWS supplies Israel's Military Intelligence Directorate with a server farm 'to store masses of intelligence information', 'the endless storage' in one source's words, which provided supplementary information before airstrikes, some of which killed civilians. Who Profits names AWS as a main cloud provider to the Israel Ministry of Defense, the military, Rafael and Israel Aerospace Industries."],"src":["SRC-V0118","SRC-V0119","SRC-W0020"]},{"h":"No exit clause","body":["Leaked Nimbus terms reported by The Guardian, +972 and Local Call in October 2025 bar Amazon from suspending Israel's access and set up a coded 'winking' payment to warn Israel of foreign data demands. When Microsoft cut off part of Unit 8200's Azure use, +972's sources said the unit planned to move the data to AWS. AWS opened its Tel Aviv region in 2023 and plans to invest $7.2B in Israel through 2037."],"src":["SRC-V0120","SRC-W0018","SRC-W0009","SRC-0045"]},{"h":"The money","body":["AWS segment sales were $42.2B in Q2 2026, up 37%. Amazon booked a $53.4B gain that quarter, mainly from its investment in Anthropic, whose Claude runs inside Palantir's Maven targeting system on AWS classified infrastructure under a 2024 partnership."],"src":["SRC-V0121","SRC-V0122","SRC-V0124"]}]},"COM-00013":{"title":"Alphabet and Google: Nimbus and Vertex","dek":"Google signed Nimbus knowing it could not control Israel's use, then rushed AI tools to the military after 7 October.","sections":[{"h":"Warned, and signed anyway","body":["A confidential Google assessment from January 2021, reported by The Intercept, warned that Nimbus 'could be used for, or linked to, the facilitation of human rights violations, including Israeli activity in the West Bank' and that Google was 'not permitted to restrict' what the Ministry of Defense and the Israeli Security Agency migrate. Google signed three months later. Leaked terms bar it from suspending service and require coded 'wink' payments to alert Israel to foreign data demands."],"src":["SRC-W0017","SRC-V0120","SRC-W0018"]},{"h":"AI for the army","body":["The Washington Post reported that Google staff expanded the Israel Ministry of Defense's access to Vertex AI in the weeks after 7 October 2023, fearing the work would otherwise go to Amazon, and that the military requested Gemini for an assistant to process documents and audio. In February 2025 Google removed language from its AI principles saying it would not use AI for weapons and surveillance, AP reported. It fired some 50 workers over Nimbus protests in 2024."],"src":["SRC-V0125","SRC-V0127","SRC-W0019"]},{"h":"The money","body":["Alphabet's Q2 2026 revenue was $119.8B; Google Cloud reached $24.77B, up 82%. In March 2026 Google completed its $32B purchase of the Israeli cybersecurity firm Wiz."],"src":["SRC-V0126","SRC-V0014"]}]},"COM-00017":{"title":"OpenAI: models for the military","dek":"OpenAI dropped its military ban, reached the Israeli army through Azure and took the Pentagon's classified work when Anthropic was blacklisted.","sections":[{"h":"Through Azure to the Israeli army","body":["OpenAI deleted its ban on 'military and warfare' use in January 2024, The Intercept reported. AP found that the Israeli military bought OpenAI models through Microsoft's Azure, and that its use of Microsoft and OpenAI AI reached nearly 200 times pre-7 October levels by March 2024, used to transcribe and translate mass-surveillance intercepts. +972 reported GPT-4 consumption via Azure rose some 20 times after October 2023."],"src":["SRC-V0131","SRC-V0127","SRC-W0008"]},{"h":"Pentagon and politics","body":["The Pentagon awarded OpenAI up to $200M in June 2025 for prototype frontier AI, including for 'warfighting'. On 27–28 February 2026, hours after Trump ordered agencies to drop Anthropic, OpenAI announced a deal to run its models on classified networks; the war on Iran began on 28 February. Microsoft owns 27% of OpenAI. President Greg Brockman and his wife gave $25M to MAGA Inc."],"src":["SRC-V0132","SRC-V0133","SRC-V0130","SRC-V0022"]}]},"COM-00019":{"title":"Oracle","dek":"Database and cloud supplier to Israel's army and the occupation's Civil Administration.","sections":[{"h":"Inside the military","body":["Who Profits reports that Oracle Israel provides software and services to the Israel Ministry of Defense and the military, where its databases 'serve logistical, intelligence and operational aspects', and supplies servers to the Civil Administration in the occupied West Bank. In January 2024, 'amid the military invasion of Gaza', it donated equipment worth more than $500,000. The Intercept reported a $319M Jerusalem data center, a confidential project with the Israeli Air Force and a 2024 AI partnership with the state weapons maker Rafael."],"src":["SRC-V0176","SRC-V0027"]},{"h":"Stated loyalty","body":["After 7 October 2023 then-CEO Safra Catz ordered 'Oracle Stands with Israel' on company screens in more than 180 countries and later said: 'if you're not for America or Israel, don't work here.' In a 2015 email she wrote 'we have to embed the love and respect for Israel in the American culture.' In July 2025 she told Calcalist: 'We are on the side of freedom. We are on the side of democracy.'"],"src":["SRC-V0027","SRC-W0031","SRC-W0034"]},{"h":"The money","body":["Oracle is an initial equity funder of Stargate, launched with Trump in January 2025, and holds 15% of TikTok's U.S. joint venture. Q1 FY2027 revenue was $19.3B, up 30%, and its contracted backlog reached $664B, up from $455B a year earlier."],"src":["SRC-W0010","SRC-V0012","SRC-W0011","SRC-W0025"]},{"h":"Military and occupation contracts","body":["Who Profits reports that Oracle Israel provides software and services to the Israel Ministry of Defense and the army, ERP and planning software to the Military Intelligence Directorate, Exadata machines to the air force since 2012, and servers to the Civil Administration in the occupied West Bank for a system that monitors 'illegal construction'. In January 2024, during the invasion of Gaza, it donated seven tons of equipment worth more than half a million dollars, Who Profits says."],"src":["SRC-V0176"]},{"h":"Cloud and AI","body":["The Intercept reports Oracle opened a $319M data centre in Jerusalem, ran a confidential four-year project with the Israeli Air Force and partnered with Rafael Advanced Defense Systems on AI. Then-CEO Safra Catz told staff: 'if you're not for America or Israel, don't work here'."],"src":["SRC-V0027"]}]},"COM-00018":{"title":"Anthropic: Claude inside Maven","dek":"Anthropic's Claude ran inside Palantir's Maven targeting system during the war on Iran.","sections":[{"h":"Into the classified kill chain","body":["In November 2024 Anthropic, Palantir and AWS agreed to give U.S. intelligence and military agencies Claude on classified networks; Anthropic also held a Pentagon contract worth up to $200M. Reuters reported that Maven 'uses multiple prompts and workflows that were built using Anthropic's Claude code.'"],"src":["SRC-V0124","SRC-V0134","SRC-W0007"]},{"h":"Minab","body":["Claude was embedded in Palantir's Maven Smart System during the 2026 Iran campaign and was reported by the Washington Post to support target generation and prioritization. Bloomberg's investigation found that Maven played a specific role in the Minab targeting chain and that overreliance on the system contributed to the strike. No public evidence currently establishes that Claude itself selected, recommended or processed the Minab school target.","The Guardian argued on 26 March 2026 that Maven's target-identification functions are older computer-vision systems and that Claude was a later natural-language layer. Dario Amodei said in June 2026: 'we don't have access to, we don't know exactly how these models were used.'"],"src":["SRC-V0309","SRC-V0348","SRC-W0003","SRC-W0024","SRC-W0012","SRC-V0349"]},{"h":"Blacklisted, still profitable","body":["On 27 February 2026 Trump ordered every federal agency to stop using Anthropic after it refused to drop limits on fully autonomous weapons and mass domestic surveillance. Its backers still profit: Amazon booked a $53.4B gain in Q2 2026 mainly from its Anthropic stake, and Microsoft a $3.2B gain."],"src":["SRC-V0236","SRC-V0134","SRC-V0122","SRC-V0129"]}]},"GOV-NEW-UNIT8200":{"title":"Unit 8200: the collection engine","dek":"Israel's signals intelligence unit feeds the kill chain with the intercepted lives of Palestinians.","sections":[{"h":"Surveillance on rented clouds","body":["Unit 8200 stored intercepted Palestinian calls on Microsoft Azure, aiming for 'a million calls an hour', with about 11,500 terabytes in the Netherlands by 2025. Sources said the data was used to plan lethal airstrikes in Gaza and arrests in the West Bank. After Microsoft disabled some services in September 2025, the unit planned to move to AWS."],"src":["SRC-W0016","SRC-W0009","SRC-V0274"]},{"h":"Its own model","body":["+972, Local Call and The Guardian reported in March 2025 that the unit was building a ChatGPT-like model trained on '100 billion words of Arabic' drawn partly from surveillance of Palestinians. 'I have more tools to know what every person in the West Bank is doing,' a source said. Al Jazeera reports Palantir has actively recruited the unit's veterans."],"src":["SRC-W0013","SRC-W0014"]}]},"PER-00022":{"title":"Alex Karp: the case file","dek":"The Palantir chief who sells targeting software to Israel's army, hunting tools to ICE and a kill chain to the Pentagon, and says so out loud: 'scare enemies and on occasion kill them.'","sections":[{"h":"ICE and deportations: the hunting software","body":["Palantir has been inside US immigration enforcement since 2013, supplying ICE with FALCON, a mobile app used during immigration raids, and Investigative Case Management (ICM). ICE renewed ICM in 2019 for $49.9M over three years; in 2022 DHS awarded a five-year ICM contract of $95.9M, which Forbes reports at $139.3M. AFSC counts $435M in ICE contracts to Palantir between 2011 and June 2026, and quotes an ICE official saying Palantir raised the agency's rate of successfully locating a target from around 27 percent to just under 80 percent.","In April 2025, as the Trump administration's mass-deportation drive began, ICE paid Palantir $30M to build ImmigrationOS, a platform to 'pull together vast amounts of data, detect patterns, and flag individuals who meet certain criteria', including tracking of so-called self-deportation. Palantir also built ELITE, which EFF describes as a map of 'potential deportation targets' with a dossier on each person and a 'confidence score' on their address, fed with addresses from the Department of Health and Human Services, which includes Medicaid. An ICE officer testified in an Oregon class action that ELITE identified arrest locations while his team worked to a verbal quota of eight arrests per day.","This is the machinery of the deadliest year in ICE detention since 2004: at least 30 people died in ICE custody in 2025. In January 2026 federal agents killed Renee Good and Alex Pretti in Minneapolis; after Pretti's killing Palantir staff wrote on internal Slack that 'ICE are the bad guys.' Karp's answer, on CNBC on 2 February 2026: 'If you are critical of ICE, you should be out there protesting for more Palantir.' Stephen Miller, the architect of the deportation policy, holds $100,000 to $250,000 of Palantir stock.","Karp has defended this work for years. In 2020 he called finding undocumented people 'a de minimis part of our work'; in December 2025 he promised to use his 'whole influence' to keep the country 'skeptical on migration' with 'a deterrent capacity'."],"src":["SRC-W0226","SRC-W0227","SRC-W0228","SRC-W0229","SRC-V0214","SRC-V0010","SRC-W0219","SRC-W0230","SRC-W0231","SRC-W0232","SRC-W0233","SRC-W0234","SRC-W0235","SRC-W0236"]},{"h":"AI targeting: Israel, Lebanon, Iran","body":["In January 2024, three months into the Gaza genocide and ethnic cleansing, Karp and Thiel held Palantir's board meeting in Tel Aviv and agreed with Israel's Ministry of Defense to 'harness Palantir's advanced technology in support of war-related missions.' Middle East Eye reported in July 2024 that Palantir's capabilities include supplying Israel's military and intelligence agencies with data 'to fire missiles at specific targets in Gaza - be it inside homes or in moving vehicles.' Al Jazeera reported in August 2026 that Palantir's software helps 'generate military targeting lists for Israeli forces.'","The UN Special Rapporteur on the occupied Palestinian territory found 'reasonable grounds to believe' Palantir provided Israel with predictive policing technology, military software infrastructure and its Artificial Intelligence Platform for 'automated decision-making', and treated Karp's 'mostly terrorists' answer as evidence of 'executive-level knowledge and purpose' regarding Israel's unlawful use of force. Palantir denies involvement in Israel's Lavender and Gospel targeting systems but says it is 'proud to support' Israeli military missions 'in other programs and contexts.'","Michael Steinberger's 2025 biography of Karp reports that Palantir technology was deployed by Israel in Lebanon in 2024, including the pager and walkie-talkie attacks, which killed dozens and wounded thousands, and that its software was used in Israeli raids in Gaza.","In Israel's war on Iran, Palantir's Maven Smart System, whose Pentagon contract ceiling was raised to nearly $1.3B, proposed 'hundreds' of targets for US strikes. Pentagon investigators, as reported by Bloomberg, found that overreliance on Maven contributed to the 28 February 2026 strike on the Shajarah Tayyebeh Elementary School in Minab, which killed at least 123 children; outdated data had catalogued the school as a Revolutionary Guard site and Maven output it as 'a recommended day-one target.' Palantir says its software was not at fault. Karp, in February 2026: 'our weapons software is in every combat situation I'm aware of.'"],"src":["SRC-0042","SRC-V0217","SRC-W0015","SRC-V0004","SRC-V0214","SRC-W0218","SRC-V0214","SRC-W0001","SRC-V0115","SRC-W0003","SRC-V0310","SRC-V0214"]},{"h":"Surveillance and data mining","body":["Palantir's business is fusing other people's data into targets. ELITE turns health-insurance addresses into arrest maps; ImmigrationOS exists to 'flag individuals'; in Israel, the UN report says, Palantir supplied 'automatic predictive policing technology', and Al Jazeera reports its software integrates 'intercepted communications, satellite imagery and other datasets'.","Karp presents this as privacy protection. 'Palantir is the most important protector of the Fourth Amendment', he told CNBC in March 2026, adding: 'The Fourth Amendment does not apply to adversaries on the battlefield.' At the DealBook Summit in December 2025 he said of surveillance data: 'Are our enemies surveilled using data that goes in our product? 100% and I completely support that.'"],"src":["SRC-W0219","SRC-W0226","SRC-V0004","SRC-W0015","SRC-W0237","SRC-W0236"]},{"h":"In his own words","body":["Karp does not hide what his product is for. On Palantir's February 2025 earnings call he said the company exists 'to scare enemies and on occasion kill them'; on the same call he said of the Trump administration's purge of the federal government: 'Some people are going to get their heads cut off.'","At a May 2024 Capitol Hill forum he said he had 'all sorts of fantasies of using drone-enabled technology to exact revenge — especially targeted — in violation of all norms', and offered pro-Palestinian campus protesters 'an exchange program sponsored by Karp. A couple months in North Korea.' The same month he said the protesters were 'praying to the altar of a new form of pagan religion.' In February 2025: 'I love the idea of getting a drone and having light fentanyl-laced urine spraying on analysts that tried to screw us.'","Asked on a November 2025 podcast about German culture, he said: 'I spend a lot of time talking to Nazis. Like, real Nazis', framing it as an effort at 'understanding what made them tick'. On staff who quit over his support for Israel: 'If you have a position that does not cost you ever to lose an employee, it's not a position.' And on his reputation: 'Being unpopular pays the bills.'"],"src":["SRC-W0217","SRC-W0238","SRC-W0239","SRC-W0240","SRC-W0241","SRC-W0242","SRC-W0243"]},{"h":"The war ideology: The Technological Republic","body":["Karp's 2025 book, written with Palantir executive Nicholas Zamiska, is a manifesto for militarising Silicon Valley. It says 'the engineering elite of Silicon Valley has an affirmative obligation to participate in the defense of the nation' and that the Pentagon must become 'an organization that can design, build, and acquire AI weaponry.' The Nation notes it calls for 'a new Manhattan Project' to keep 'the most sophisticated forms of AI for the battlefield' and suggests 'moving away from an all-volunteer force'.","In 2024 he told The New York Times: 'I'm not apologizing for giving our product to Ukraine or Israel or lots of other places.'"],"src":["SRC-W0244","SRC-W0245","SRC-W0246"]},{"h":"The money","body":["Palantir's 2026 proxy reports Karp's 2025 compensation at $8.62M, including $2.51M for personal security and $2.00M for aircraft, up from $4.36M in 2024. That is small change: he sold more than $2B of Palantir stock in 2024, and Forbes puts his fortune at $20.2B (5 Oct 2026). He gave $1M to Trump's super PAC MAGA Inc in December 2024 and $3.8M to federal candidates from 2006 to February 2026. The US Army has consolidated Palantir software deals into a contract worth up to $10B over ten years."],"src":["SRC-V0211","SRC-V0212","SRC-W0133","SRC-V0213","SRC-V0214","SRC-V0116"]}],"quotes":[{"text":"Palantir is here to disrupt and make the institutions we partner with the very best in the world and, when it's necessary, to scare enemies and on occasion kill them","who":"Alex Karp, Palantir Q4 2024 earnings call, February 2025","src":"SRC-W0217"},{"text":"mostly terrorists, that's true","who":"Alex Karp, to a protester, Hill & Valley Forum, 30 April 2025 (as recorded in UN report A/HRC/59/23)","src":"SRC-V0004"},{"text":"I am proud that we are supporting Israel in every way we can","who":"Alex Karp, CNBC, November 2023","src":"SRC-V0214"},{"text":"I had all sorts of fantasies of using drone-enabled technology to exact revenge — especially targeted — in violation of all norms.","who":"Alex Karp, Capitol Hill forum, 1 May 2024","src":"SRC-W0238"},{"text":"We're gonna do an exchange program sponsored by Karp. A couple months in North Korea, nice-tasting flavored bark.","who":"Alex Karp, on campus protesters, Capitol Hill forum, 1 May 2024","src":"SRC-W0238"},{"text":"They are literally praying to the altar of a new form of pagan religion","who":"Alex Karp, on campus protesters, AI Expo, Washington, May 2024","src":"SRC-W0239"},{"text":"I love the idea of getting a drone and having light fentanyl-laced urine spraying on analysts that tried to screw us","who":"Alex Karp, February 2025","src":"SRC-W0240"},{"text":"If you are critical of ICE, you should be out there protesting for more Palantir.","who":"Alex Karp, CNBC, 2 February 2026","src":"SRC-W0233"},{"text":"I spend a lot of time talking to Nazis. Like, real Nazis.","who":"Alex Karp, podcast interview with Molly O'Shea, November 2025","src":"SRC-W0241"},{"text":"The Fourth Amendment does not apply to adversaries on the battlefield.","who":"Alex Karp, CNBC, 12 March 2026","src":"SRC-W0237"}]},"PER-00023":{"title":"Peter Thiel","dek":"Palantir's chairman and JD Vance's patron.","sections":[{"h":"Record","body":["Peter Thiel co-founded Palantir and has chaired it since 2003; with Karp he met Israeli officials in Tel Aviv in January 2024 when Palantir agreed its war partnership. In 2009 he wrote: 'I no longer believe that freedom and democracy are compatible.'"],"src":["SRC-0042","SRC-W0028"]},{"h":"Money and politics","body":["He still holds about 3% of Palantir, worth more than $10B, per the Motley Fool (Aug 2026); Forbes puts his net worth at $37.7B. Vice President JD Vance worked for Thiel's fund Mithril Capital, and Multinationals Observatory documents former Palantir staff placed across the Trump administration."],"src":["SRC-V0215","SRC-V0216","SRC-W0029"]}]},"PER-00004":{"title":"Larry Ellison","dek":"Oracle's founder: $16.6M to Israel's army charity, a board seat offered to Netanyahu.","sections":[{"h":"For the Israeli army","body":["Larry Ellison gave $16.6M to Friends of the Israel Defense Forces in 2017, then the largest single gift in its history, and more than $26M in total, Responsible Statecraft reported. The Intercept reported he once offered Benjamin Netanyahu a seat on Oracle's board. Oracle supplies data systems to the Israeli military, per Who Profits."],"src":["SRC-0048","SRC-W0031","SRC-V0027","SRC-V0176"]},{"h":"For Trump","body":["Ellison gave about $45M to a nonprofit backing Trump's 2024 campaign, Fortune reported, then stood beside Trump on 21 January 2025 to launch the $500B Stargate project. Oracle took 15% of TikTok's U.S. joint venture; Responsible Statecraft noted the owners gain control of the algorithms that shape what U.S. users see. His son David's Paramount Skydance won shareholder approval for a $110B Warner Bros. Discovery merger, including CNN, in April 2026. Forbes puts his net worth at $184.2B."],"src":["SRC-W0030","SRC-V0023","SRC-V0012","SRC-W0035","SRC-V0013","SRC-V0174"]},{"h":"Money","body":["Forbes puts Ellison's net worth at $184.2B, built on about 40% of Oracle. He gave $16.6M to Friends of the IDF in November 2017, then the largest single gift in its history, for facilities on a new infantry training campus. Wikipedia's FIDF entry puts his total giving to the charity at $25M."],"src":["SRC-V0174","SRC-0048","SRC-V0184"]},{"h":"Netanyahu","body":["In August 2021 Benjamin Netanyahu holidayed on Lanai, the Hawaiian island Ellison owns, while Ellison was a prosecution witness in Netanyahu's corruption trial. Citing Haaretz, the Times of Israel reported in September 2021 that Ellison offered Netanyahu an Oracle board seat; Netanyahu denied receiving the offer."],"src":["SRC-W0053","SRC-W0055"]},{"h":"Oracle's Israel business","body":["Who Profits reports Oracle supplies the Israel Ministry of Defense, army and air force and servers to the Civil Administration in the occupied West Bank. Ellison launched the $500B Stargate AI project alongside Donald Trump in January 2025."],"src":["SRC-V0176","SRC-V0023"]}]},"PER-00024":{"title":"Jeff Bezos","dek":"Amazon founder whose cloud runs Israel's military intelligence data.","sections":[{"h":"Record","body":["Jeff Bezos founded Amazon and chairs its board. AWS holds half of Project Nimbus and, per +972 and Local Call, gives Israel's Military Intelligence Directorate a server farm for 'masses of intelligence information' used ahead of airstrikes. Leaked terms bar Amazon from cutting Israel off."],"src":["SRC-V0119","SRC-V0118","SRC-V0120"]},{"h":"Money and politics","body":["Forbes puts his net worth at $371.2B (Oct 2026). After the 2024 election Amazon gave $1M to Trump's inauguration, which Bezos attended, after he blocked The Washington Post's endorsement of Kamala Harris."],"src":["SRC-V0218","SRC-V0219"]},{"h":"Role","body":["Forbes puts Bezos at $371.2B. AWS and Google run Project Nimbus, Israel's $1.2B government cloud, and +972 Magazine reported AWS hosts intelligence for the army's operations in Gaza."],"src":["SRC-V0218","SRC-V0119","SRC-V0004"]}]},"PER-00030":{"title":"Sam Altman","dek":"OpenAI's CEO opened its models to the military and aligned with Trump.","sections":[{"h":"Record","body":["Under Sam Altman OpenAI dropped its military-use ban in January 2024; AP found the Israeli military's use of Microsoft and OpenAI AI reached nearly 200 times pre-7 October levels by March 2024. Hours after Trump blacklisted Anthropic in February 2026, Altman announced OpenAI's classified Pentagon deal."],"src":["SRC-V0131","SRC-V0127","SRC-V0133"]},{"h":"Politics","body":["He gave $1M to Trump's inaugural committee and at the Stargate launch said: 'I think if it were a different President, it might not have been possible.' Forbes puts his net worth at $3.3B and says he holds no OpenAI equity."],"src":["SRC-V0233","SRC-V0023","SRC-V0232"]}]},"PER-N-TRUMP":{"title":"Donald Trump: The Arsonist in Chief","dek":"He armed Israel's Gaza genocide and ethnic cleansing, launched the war on Iran alongside Israel, threatened to wipe out a civilization, punished the court and the investigator who documented the crimes, and banked the money that flowed in from those who wanted it all.","sections":[{"h":"Count one: threatening to erase a civilization","body":["On April 7, 2026, hours before his deadline to Iran, Donald Trump posted on Truth Social: \"A whole civilization will die tonight, never to be brought back again. I don't want that to happen, but it probably will,\" according to ABC News and CBS News. ABC News reported that a day earlier he said the entire country \"could be taken out in one night\", and that he had told ABC on the Sunday: \"we're blowing up the entire country.\" On Easter Sunday he announced \"Power Plant Day, and Bridge Day\" for Iran, cursing at its people to \"Open the Fuckin' Strait\", as the Lowy Institute recorded. A two-week ceasefire was reached before the deadline, according to CBS News and NBC News.","The threats came after the US-Israeli strike campaign that began on February 28, 2026. When a strike destroyed a girls' elementary school in Minab, Iranian media reported 175 dead, \"many of them children\", per PolitiFact. Trump first blamed Iran, telling reporters it was \"done by Iran\" because Iranian munitions were \"very inaccurate\", Al Jazeera reported. Two days later he claimed \"Iran also has some Tomahawks\", the missile experts linked to the strike. PolitiFact rated it False: no combatant but the US is known to hold Tomahawks, and the White House offered no evidence."],"src":["SRC-W0185","SRC-W0186","SRC-W0187","SRC-W0188","SRC-W0189"]},{"h":"Count two: Gaza as a property deal, Lebanon as a footnote","body":["Standing beside Benjamin Netanyahu, a man wanted by the International Criminal Court, Trump announced on February 4, 2025: \"The US will take over the Gaza Strip and we will do a job with it, too. We'll own it.\" He promised \"the Riviera of the Middle East\" and said Gaza's people should \"go to other countries of interest with humanitarian hearts\", Al Jazeera reported. That is a plan for ethnic cleansing, delivered as a sales pitch.","Three weeks later, on February 26, 2025, he posted an AI-generated video to Truth Social and Instagram showing a golden statue of himself on a Gaza street and himself lounging shirtless with Netanyahu, to a soundtrack of \"Trump Gaza is finally here\" and \"Trump Gaza number one\", Global News reported. Before taking office he had already promised \"ALL HELL TO PAY in the Middle East\" (Truth Social, December 2, 2024, per The Times of Israel).","South Lebanon received even less regard. On April 8, 2026, as Lebanese health authorities counted at least 254 people killed in a single day of Israeli strikes, Trump called it a \"separate skirmish\" and told PBS: \"Because of Hezbollah, they were not included in the deal,\" Al Jazeera reported."],"src":["SRC-W0190","SRC-W0191","SRC-W0192","SRC-W0193"]},{"h":"Count three: lies on the fact-checkers' record","body":["The case for the war on Iran rested on claims the fact-checkers took apart. FactCheck.org found no evidence for Trump's assertion that Iran \"attempted to rebuild\" its nuclear program, quoting arms control expert Daryl Kimball, and called his line that Iran would \"soon\" have missiles reaching the US misleading; the Defense Intelligence Agency projection cited put that at 2035. After his April 2026 prime-time address, FactCheck.org again noted that the sites he said were \"totally obliterated\" in 2025 were damaged but not destroyed. In June 2025 he had attacked CNN and The New York Times for reporting exactly that.","PolitiFact rated False his October 2026 claim that he had always meant \"4-6 weeks\" only for the nuclear threat, finding he \"did not distinguish\" that from the length of the war. His August 2026 boasts that the US was \"doing extremely well\" were weighed against a cost of \"at least $37.5 billion\" and 18 dead US service members.","On Gaza aid, his July 27, 2025 claim \"We gave $60 million two weeks ago\" for food drew a Half True: PolitiFact found a \"$30 million grant in late June\" that \"as of July 26\" had not been distributed."],"src":["SRC-W0194","SRC-W0195","SRC-W0196","SRC-W0197","SRC-W0198","SRC-W0199"]},{"h":"Count four: the donor who sat in the Knesset gallery","body":["Miriam Adelson gave $100 million to Preserve America, the super PAC backing Trump in 2024, the Jewish Telegraphic Agency reported, a sum that \"dwarfed the $75 million\" from Elon Musk. JTA recorded that a confidant said she opposes a Palestinian state, while her spokesperson denied she had made Trump's backing of West Bank annexation a condition of her support.","Trump repaid the attention in public. In the Knesset on October 13, 2025, he pointed her out: \"She's got US$60 billion (S$77.8 billion) in the bank,\" Reuters reported via AsiaOne, and he credited the Adelsons with shaping his decisions on Jerusalem, the embassy move and the occupied Golan Heights. He told the chamber he had asked her: \"What do you love more, the United States or Israel?\" and that \"She refused to answer.\" At the White House Hanukkah event on December 16, 2025, he said of a third term: \"She said, 'Think about it, I'll give you another $250 million,'\" Newsweek reported. The donor got the policies she wanted. Trump kept saying so out loud."],"src":["SRC-W0200","SRC-W0201","SRC-W0202","SRC-W0203"]},{"h":"Count five: the family business in the Gulf","body":["Jared Kushner, the son-in-law who helped broker the October 2025 Gaza ceasefire as an envoy, runs Affinity Partners. In a March 19, 2026 letter, ranking Democrats Robert Garcia and Ron Wyden wrote that Affinity managed about $3 billion in foreign money, \"including $2 billion from the Saudi Public Investment Fund\", and took about $157 million in fees, $87 million of it directly from the Saudi government. The letter also named the Qatar Investment Authority as a source. CNN reported on October 1, 2026, as relayed by the Daily Caller, that Affinity held a \"7.4% stake worth more than $1 billion\" in Israel's Phoenix Financial, which invested in suppliers to the Israeli military, including Elbit Systems.","The Trump Organization's own regional deals followed. Qatar's state-owned Qatari Diar and the Saudi developer Dar Global announced a $5.5 billion Trump golf resort in Qatar on April 30, 2025 (Newsweek). Dar Global announced a $1 billion Trump-branded project in Saudi Arabia in September 2025, after Trump Tower Jeddah (PBS NewsHour). Qatar's government gave Trump a Boeing 747-8, unveiled as Air Force One on June 19, 2026 and reported by CBS News as a $400 million plane."],"src":["SRC-W0204","SRC-W0204","SRC-W0205","SRC-W0206","SRC-W0207","SRC-W0208"]},{"h":"Count six: the bomb pipeline","body":["Days into his term, Trump released the 2,000-pound bombs the previous administration had held back over their use in densely populated Gaza. \"We released them. We released them today. And they'll have them. They paid for them,\" he told reporters on Air Force One, The Times of Israel reported on January 26, 2025.","By March 1, 2025, Secretary of State Marco Rubio had used emergency authority to rush about $4 billion in military assistance to Israel, and said the administration had approved \"nearly $12 billion in major foreign military sales to Israel\" since January 20, VOA reported. A week into the war on Iran, Rubio again waived congressional review to sell Israel more than 20,000 bombs worth around $650 million, Reuters reported via The Times of Israel on March 8, 2026. Representative Gregory Meeks called it \"an emergency of the Trump administration's own creation.\"","A tally of notifications to Congress and State Department data published by The New Arab on September 25, 2026 puts the arms deals Trump approved for Israel between January 20, 2025 and September 17, 2026 at $21.6 billion, including $7.4 billion in February 2025, $6.67 billion in January 2026 and a $2.8 billion package in September 2026."],"src":["SRC-W0209","SRC-W0210","SRC-V0037","SRC-W0211"]},{"h":"Count seven: punishing the witnesses","body":["Trump went after those who documented the crimes. Executive Order 14203 of February 6, 2025 sanctioned the International Criminal Court for \"illegitimate and baseless actions targeting America and our close ally Israel\" and attacked the \"baseless arrest warrants\" for Netanyahu and Yoav Gallant. Prosecutor Karim Khan was listed immediately, and on June 5, 2025 four judges were added, two of whom had approved those warrants, Verfassungsblog reported. On July 9, 2025, Rubio sanctioned UN Special Rapporteur Francesca Albanese, the author of a report on companies profiting from the genocide, over what he called a \"campaign of political and economic warfare against the United States and Israel\". She dismissed it as \"mafia style intimidation techniques\", Al Jazeera reported.","At home, he celebrated the March 2025 ICE detention of Mahmoud Khalil, a green card holder who was not charged with any crime: \"This is the first arrest of many to come... We will find, apprehend, and deport these terrorist sympathizers,\" he posted. Abroad, he wrote to Israel's president on November 12, 2025: \"I hereby call on you to fully pardon Benjamin Netanyahu\", who is on trial for bribery, fraud and breach of trust."],"src":["SRC-W0212","SRC-W0213","SRC-W0214","SRC-W0215","SRC-W0216"]}],"quotes":[{"text":"Tuesday will be Power Plant Day, and Bridge Day, all wrapped up in one, in Iran. There will be nothing like it!!! Open the Fuckin' Strait, you crazy bastards, or you'll be living in Hell – JUST WATCH!","who":"Donald Trump, Truth Social post, Easter Sunday, April 5, 2026","src":"SRC-W0187"},{"text":"The US will take over the Gaza Strip and we will do a job with it, too. We'll own it","who":"Donald Trump, White House press conference with Benjamin Netanyahu, February 4, 2025 (reported February 5, 2025)","src":"SRC-W0190"},{"text":"Based on what I've seen, that was done by Iran. We think it was done by Iran – because they are very inaccurate, as you know, with their munitions.","who":"Donald Trump, to reporters on the Minab girls' school strike, Saturday, March 7, 2026","src":"SRC-W0188"},{"text":"Iran also has some Tomahawks.","who":"Donald Trump, press conference, March 9, 2026 (rated False by PolitiFact)","src":"SRC-W0189"},{"text":"if the hostages are not released prior to January 20, 2025, the date that I proudly assume Office as President of the United States, there will be ALL HELL TO PAY in the Middle East","who":"Donald Trump, Truth Social post, December 2, 2024","src":"SRC-W0192"},{"text":"Because of Hezbollah, they were not included in the deal.","who":"Donald Trump, to PBS, April 8, 2026, the day Israeli strikes killed at least 254 people in Lebanon; he called Lebanon a \"separate skirmish\"","src":"SRC-W0193"},{"text":"So Miriam, I know you love Israel. What do you love more, the United States or Israel?","who":"Donald Trump, speech to the Knesset, October 13, 2025, singling out megadonor Miriam Adelson","src":"SRC-W0202"},{"text":"This is the first arrest of many to come.","who":"Donald Trump, Truth Social post on the ICE detention of Columbia graduate Mahmoud Khalil, March 10, 2025","src":"SRC-W0215"},{"text":"I hereby call on you to fully pardon Benjamin Netanyahu","who":"Donald Trump, letter to Israeli President Isaac Herzog, November 12, 2025","src":"SRC-W0216"}],"lead":{"text":"A whole civilization will die tonight, never to be brought back again. I don't want that to happen, but it probably will","who":"Donald Trump, Truth Social post, April 7, 2026 (ahead of his deadline to Iran)","src":"SRC-W0185"}},"case11":{"title":"The Corporate Zionists and War Profiteers Behind Israel's Apartheid Regime","dek":"Asset managers that own the arms makers, CEOs paid $23M–$38M a year as the bombs ship, and billionaires who fund Israel's army, its institutions and the politicians who shield it. Every figure is sourced to filings, UN records or named reporting.","sections":[{"h":"The system, not a list","body":["Israel's assault on Gaza runs on money from far outside Israel. In July 2025 UN Special Rapporteur Francesca Albanese published 'From economy of occupation to economy of genocide' (A/HRC/59/23), which names more than 60 companies, from arms makers to cloud providers and the asset managers that own them. 'What I expose is not a list, it is a system,' she said, and: 'Clearly, for some, genocide is profitable.'","In September 2025 the UN Independent International Commission of Inquiry concluded that Israel has committed genocide in Gaza, finding four of the five acts defined by the 1948 Genocide Convention. It urged all states to stop arms transfers and to 'ensure individuals and corporations in their territories and within their jurisdiction are not involved in aiding and assisting the commission of genocide.' A year earlier, on 19 July 2024, the International Court of Justice found Israel's continued presence in the occupied Palestinian territory unlawful and said states must not 'render aid or assistance in maintaining the situation'. Human Rights Watch concluded in 2021 that Israeli authorities are committing the crimes against humanity of apartheid and persecution.","This file follows the money through three channels: the funds that own the weapons industry, the executives paid to run it, and the billionaires who give directly to Israel's army, its institutions and its political protectors."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-W0052","SRC-V0025","SRC-W0056","SRC-W0057"]},{"h":"BlackRock and Larry Fink: owner of the arsenal","body":["BlackRock, co-founded in 1988 by Larry Fink, Robert Kapito, Susan Wagner and five others, is the world's largest asset manager, with a record $15.3T under management in Q2 2026. The UN report names it the second-largest institutional investor in Palantir (8.6%), Microsoft (7.8%), Amazon (6.6%), Alphabet (6.6%) and IBM (8.6%), and the third-largest in Lockheed Martin (7.2%) and Caterpillar (7.5%). The same report records BlackRock buying $68M of the Israeli treasury bonds that, in the rapporteur's words, 'played a critical role in funding the ongoing assault on Gaza.'","Its 2026 filings show the stake has only grown in value: 110.5M RTX shares worth $21.0B, 61.0M Boeing shares worth $13.2B, 18.9M Lockheed Martin shares worth $9.6B and 8.3% of Northrop Grumman at 30 June 2026. Six days after 7 October 2023 Fink told CNBC that Israel was 'going to have to be spending a lot more money on military' and that 'we are not seeing anybody looking to divest from Israel for political reasons.' The Jerusalem Post, in a September 2026 profile, describes his 'deeply personal support for Israel' and reports that BlackRock 'has also opposed anti-Israel BDS-aligned resolutions'. BlackRock paid Fink $37.7M for 2025; Forbes values him at $1.3B."],"src":["SRC-W0058","SRC-V0151","SRC-V0004","SRC-0056","SRC-V0162","SRC-W0059","SRC-V0157","SRC-V0160"]},{"h":"Vanguard, State Street, Fidelity, Capital Group: the rest of the cartel","body":["Vanguard, run since July 2024 by former BlackRock executive Salim Ramji, is named by the UN report as the largest institutional investor in Caterpillar (9.8%), whose bulldozers demolish Palestinian homes, Chevron (8.9%) and Palantir (9.1%), and second-largest in Lockheed Martin (9.2%) and Elbit Systems (2.0%). It bought $546M of Israeli treasury bonds. In 2026 SEC filings it holds 6.88% to 7.47% of each of RTX, Boeing, Northrop Grumman, Lockheed Martin and General Dynamics.","State Street, chaired by Ronald O'Hanley, is the largest single owner of Lockheed Martin, with about 14.3% ($16.8B) at 30 June 2026, plus 9.27% of Northrop Grumman. Fidelity, controlled by the Johnson family and run by Abigail Johnson, held 7.0% of Boeing at the end of 2025, and its donor-advised fund passed $2.2M to Friends of the IDF, according to Rolling Stone. Capital Group, founded in 1931 by Jonathan Bell Lovelace, is the largest shareholder of BAE Systems (11.04% in January 2026), which builds part of every F-35, and holds about 5% of both Boeing and Northrop Grumman.","Between them, BlackRock and Vanguard alone held 16.4% of Lockheed Martin and 17.3% of Caterpillar at the time of the UN report. Index funds turn this into automatic policy: every pension contribution buys more of the companies arming a state the UN Commission of Inquiry says has committed genocide."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-V0030","SRC-0055","SRC-0060","SRC-0068","SRC-W0050","SRC-0057","SRC-0066","SRC-0070","SRC-V0172","SRC-V0155","SRC-0072","SRC-0069","SRC-0067"]},{"h":"The arms-maker bosses: paid as the bombs ship","body":["The CEOs of the five big US weapons makers each took home $23M–$26M for 2025, according to company proxies: Phebe Novakovic of General Dynamics $25.9M, Kathy Warden of Northrop Grumman $25.4M, Christopher Calio of RTX $24.8M, Kelly Ortberg of Boeing $23.6M and James Taiclet of Lockheed Martin $23.5M. Novakovic took $72.3M and Taiclet $70.0M over 2023–2025.","Their products are documented in Gaza. Lockheed's F-35 is, in the UN rapporteur's words, 'the largest-ever defence procurement programme', and Campaign Against Arms Trade confirmed an Israeli F-35 carried out the 13 July 2024 strike on the al-Mawasi 'safe zone' that killed 90 people. Northrop Grumman builds the centre fuselage of every F-35, including Israel's. Boeing was named lead contractor in a $6.75B package of bombs and JDAM guidance kits notified in February 2025; General Dynamics was a prime contractor on the $2.04B bomb-body sale approved under an emergency declaration that month. RTX co-produces Israel's interceptors and its Pratt & Whitney unit builds the F-35's engines.","The executives said the quiet part on earnings calls. In October 2023 Taiclet pointed investors to 'supplemental requests related to support Ukraine, Israel', Warden noted 'supplemental requests for urgent needs, including those in Ukraine and Israel', and then-RTX CEO Greg Hayes said 'across the entire Raytheon portfolio, you're going to see a benefit of this restocking.' During the 2026 US–Israeli war on Iran, Taiclet called the moment 'a golden opportunity'."],"src":["SRC-0115","SRC-W0046","SRC-0117","SRC-0118","SRC-0116","SRC-V0004","SRC-V0192","SRC-W0048","SRC-0027","SRC-0039","SRC-V0193","SRC-V0194"]},{"h":"Billionaires who give to the army and its institutions","body":["Some donors skip the stock market and give directly. Oracle co-founder Larry Ellison gave $16.6M to Friends of the Israel Defense Forces (FIDF) at a November 2017 gala, then the largest single gift in its history, for facilities on a new infantry training campus; he said there was 'no greater honor than supporting' Israel's soldiers. Home Depot co-founder Bernie Marcus, who died in November 2024, gave FIDF nearly $13M between 2009 and 2022 and, Forbes reported, backed the IDF and Magen David Adom after 7 October 2023. FIDF's revenue tripled from $89.3M in 2022 to $282.0M in 2023.","Michael Bloomberg matched about $44M from 34,000 donors in October 2023 for an $88M gift to Magen David Adom, Israel's national emergency service, buying ambulances, ballistic vests and helmets, and calling Israel 'our ally'. Bloomberg Philanthropies then committed $27.8M to 65 Israeli towns in the south and north. Blackstone president Jonathan Gray and his wife gave $125M to Tel Aviv University's medical faculty in May 2025, the largest gift in its history, saying they wanted to 'show up and say we support you'. Blackstone CEO Stephen Schwarzman gave $10M to the National Library of Israel in 2018 and in May 2024 backed Donald Trump, citing 'the dramatic rise of antisemitism'.","Gifts like these flowed in the same months as Israel's assault on Gaza, and no comparable public gift by these donors to Palestinian medical services was found. Their money builds the home front of a war the UN Commission of Inquiry calls genocide."],"src":["SRC-0048","SRC-V0184","SRC-V0183","SRC-W0054","SRC-W0037","SRC-W0036","SRC-W0038","SRC-W0040","SRC-W0041","SRC-W0043","SRC-W0044"]},{"h":"Big Tech: cloud, AI and censorship","body":["The UN report names Microsoft, Alphabet, Amazon, IBM and Palantir among firms that supply Israel's military and surveillance systems, and BlackRock and Vanguard among their biggest owners. Amazon Web Services and Google run Project Nimbus, Israel's $1.2B government cloud contract; +972 Magazine and Local Call reported AWS provides Israel's Military Intelligence Directorate with a server farm used 'to store masses of intelligence information that assists the army in the war'. Amazon founder Jeff Bezos is worth $371.2B according to Forbes.","Oracle, about 40% owned by Larry Ellison, supplies software to the Israel Ministry of Defense, the army and air force, and servers to the Civil Administration in the occupied West Bank, according to Who Profits. The Intercept reports Oracle built a $319M data centre in Jerusalem and worked with Rafael on an AI project. Human Rights Watch reviewed 1,050 cases in December 2023 and found Meta's moderation had 'increasingly silenced voices in support of Palestine on Instagram and Facebook'."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-V0119","SRC-V0218","SRC-V0176","SRC-V0027","SRC-V0174","SRC-W0060"]},{"h":"How they get away with it","body":["The same fortunes fund US politics. Miriam Adelson was the third-largest donor to Donald Trump's 2024 campaign at $106M and sought his support for annexing the occupied West Bank; Paul Singer gave $5M; Schwarzman publicly endorsed him; Bernie Marcus gave $2M to AIPAC in 2023, according to Wikipedia's account. On the Democratic side Haim Saban, who calls himself 'a one-issue guy, and my issue is Israel', has been among the party's largest donors. Larry Ellison stood beside Trump to launch the $500B Stargate project the day after the 2025 inauguration, and the Times of Israel reported, citing Haaretz, that he offered Benjamin Netanyahu a seat on Oracle's board in 2021, while Ellison was a prosecution witness in Netanyahu's corruption trial.","Asset managers call their holdings passive. But they vote the shares, they bought the war bonds, and their chiefs speak openly about rising military budgets. Under the Genocide Convention and the ICJ's 2024 opinion, the UN Commission of Inquiry and the Special Rapporteur say states must stop corporations under their jurisdiction from aiding these crimes. So far none of the firms or donors in this file has divested or stopped giving."],"src":["SRC-V0021","SRC-V0182","SRC-W0044","SRC-V0183","SRC-V0186","SRC-V0023","SRC-W0055","SRC-W0053","SRC-V0025","SRC-W0056","SRC-V0004"]}],"quotes":[{"text":"One people enriched, one people erased. Clearly, for some, genocide is profitable.","who":"Francesca Albanese, UN Special Rapporteur on the occupied Palestinian territory, July 2025","src":"SRC-W0052"},{"text":"Israel has committed genocide against Palestinians in the Gaza Strip","who":"UN Independent International Commission of Inquiry, 16 Sept 2025","src":"SRC-V0025"},{"text":"They're going to have to be spending a lot more money on military.","who":"Larry Fink, BlackRock chairman and CEO, on Israel, CNBC, 13 Oct 2023","src":"SRC-V0162"},{"text":"We are not seeing anybody looking to divest from Israel for political reasons.","who":"Larry Fink, CNBC, 13 Oct 2023","src":"SRC-V0162"},{"text":"I think really across the entire Raytheon portfolio, you're going to see a benefit of this restocking.","who":"Greg Hayes, then RTX CEO, October 2023","src":"SRC-V0193"},{"text":"In any of these scenarios, there continues to be the option also for supplemental requests related to support Ukraine, Israel, and potentially Taiwan.","who":"James Taiclet, Lockheed Martin CEO, October 2023","src":"SRC-V0193"},{"text":"As we saw last week, the administration continues to make supplemental requests for urgent needs, including those in Ukraine and Israel.","who":"Kathy Warden, Northrop Grumman CEO, October 2023","src":"SRC-V0193"},{"text":"In my mind, there is no greater honor than supporting some of the bravest people in the world, and I thank FIDF for allowing us to celebrate and support these soldiers year after year.","who":"Larry Ellison, on his $16.6M gift to Friends of the IDF, Nov 2017","src":"SRC-0048"},{"text":"if you're not for America or Israel, don't work here—this is a free country.","who":"Safra Catz, then Oracle CEO, as reported by The Intercept","src":"SRC-V0027"},{"text":"America has always been a friend to Israel and I am encouraged that so many of us are stepping up to help our ally during these challenging times.","who":"Michael Bloomberg, Oct 2023","src":"SRC-W0037"},{"text":"we certainly have a huge amount of sympathy for the people of Israel and we just wanted to show up and say 'we support you and we want to help you'","who":"Jonathan Gray, Blackstone president, May 2025","src":"SRC-W0040"},{"text":"The dramatic rise of antisemitism has led me to focus on the consequences of upcoming elections with greater urgency.","who":"Stephen Schwarzman, Blackstone CEO, backing Donald Trump, May 2024","src":"SRC-W0044"}]},"case01":{"sections":[{"h":"What the UN Special Rapporteur found","body":["UN Special Rapporteur Francesca Albanese's July 2025 report A/HRC/59/23 names BlackRock and Vanguard among the largest institutional investors in companies it ties to Israel's occupation and genocide. BlackRock is second-largest in Palantir (8.6%), Microsoft (7.8%), Amazon (6.6%), Alphabet (6.6%) and IBM (8.6%), and third-largest in Lockheed Martin (7.2%) and Caterpillar (7.5%). Vanguard is largest in Caterpillar (9.8%), Chevron (8.9%) and Palantir (9.1%), and second-largest in Lockheed Martin (9.2%) and Elbit Systems (2.0%).","The report says that through these funds the asset managers 'implicate universities, pension funds and ordinary people who passively invest their savings'. In September 2025 the UN Commission of Inquiry found Israel has committed genocide and called on states to ensure corporations are 'not involved in aiding and assisting the commission of genocide'."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-V0025"]},{"h":"The same five owners, every weapons maker","body":["2026 filings show five firms among the top owners of every big US arms maker. State Street holds about 14.3% of Lockheed Martin and 9.27% of Northrop Grumman; BlackRock 8.2% of RTX, 8.3% of Northrop and 7.7% of Boeing; Vanguard between 6.88% and 7.47% of each; Fidelity 7.0% of Boeing; Capital Group 5.2% of Boeing and Northrop and 11.04% of BAE Systems. Longview Asset Management filed in 2026 that it held about 10% of General Dynamics."],"src":["SRC-0057","SRC-0066","SRC-0056","SRC-0060","SRC-0068","SRC-0070","SRC-0069","SRC-0067","SRC-0072","SRC-0074"]}]},"case03":{"sections":[{"h":"Billionaire money behind Israel's political shield","body":["Bernie Marcus gave $2M to AIPAC in 2023, according to Wikipedia's account; Fidelity Charitable passed $2.7M to AIPAC's educational affiliate, Rolling Stone reported. Miriam Adelson gave $106M to Donald Trump's 2024 campaign and sought his support for annexing the occupied West Bank; Paul Singer gave $5M and sits on the board of the Republican Jewish Coalition. Blackstone CEO Stephen Schwarzman endorsed Trump in May 2024, citing antisemitism. Haim Saban, among the Democratic Party's largest donors, describes Israel as his one issue."],"src":["SRC-V0183","SRC-V0172","SRC-V0021","SRC-V0182","SRC-W0044","SRC-V0186"]}]},"case04":{"sections":[{"h":"Wall Street in Elbit","body":["The UN Special Rapporteur's 2025 report names Vanguard the second-largest institutional investor in Elbit Systems, Israel's largest private arms maker, at 2.0% of its shares. Fidelity's Contrafund held $4M of Elbit stock in January 2026."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-V0171"]}]},"case05":{"sections":[{"h":"The owners of Israel's cloud and AI suppliers","body":["The UN report names BlackRock the second-largest institutional investor in Microsoft (7.8%), Amazon (6.6%), Alphabet (6.6%), IBM (8.6%) and Palantir (8.6%), and Vanguard the largest in Palantir (9.1%). Oracle, chaired by Larry Ellison, supplies the Israel Ministry of Defense, army and air force and servers to the Civil Administration in the occupied West Bank, according to Who Profits; The Intercept reports a $319M Oracle data centre in Jerusalem and a Rafael AI partnership. Human Rights Watch found in December 2023, after reviewing 1,050 cases, that Meta had 'increasingly silenced voices in support of Palestine on Instagram and Facebook'."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-V0176","SRC-V0027","SRC-W0060"]}]},"case06":{"sections":[{"h":"What the CEOs told investors","body":["In October 2023, then-RTX CEO Greg Hayes told investors that 'across the entire Raytheon portfolio, you're going to see a benefit of this restocking'; Lockheed Martin's James Taiclet pointed to 'supplemental requests related to support Ukraine, Israel'; Northrop Grumman's Kathy Warden noted 'supplemental requests for urgent needs, including those in Ukraine and Israel.' BlackRock's Larry Fink said Israel would 'have to be spending a lot more money on military.' In April 2026, during the US–Israeli war on Iran, Taiclet called it 'a golden opportunity'."],"src":["SRC-V0193","SRC-V0162","SRC-V0194"]}]},"case07":{"sections":[{"h":"US funds own Europe's F-35 parts maker","body":["Capital Group is the largest shareholder of BAE Systems, with 11.04% of voting rights in January 2026, and data providers list BlackRock at about 9.5% in July 2026. BAE builds the rear fuselage of every F-35, the jet used in the July 2024 al-Mawasi strike that killed 90 people."],"src":["SRC-0072","SRC-0071","SRC-V0192"]}]},"case08":{"sections":[{"h":"War bonds: who financed the military budget","body":["The UN Special Rapporteur reports that Israel's military budget grew from 4.2% to 8.3% of GDP between 2022 and 2024, driving a 6.8% deficit. Israel covered it with bond issues including $8B in March 2024 and $5B in February 2025. Asset managers were among at least 400 buyers from 36 countries: PIMCO $960M, Vanguard $546M and BlackRock $68M. The Development Corporation for Israel (Israel Bonds) tripled annual sales to send nearly $5B to Israel since October 2023."],"src":["SRC-V0004"]}]},"case09":{"sections":[{"h":"Paid to arm a genocide","body":["2025 pay, per company proxies: Phebe Novakovic (General Dynamics) $25.9M, Kathy Warden (Northrop Grumman) $25.4M, Christopher Calio (RTX) $24.8M, Kelly Ortberg (Boeing) $23.6M, James Taiclet (Lockheed Martin) $23.5M. At BlackRock, the largest owner across the sector, Larry Fink took $37.7M and president Robert Kapito $25.7M. Northrop disclosed Warden's pay as 224 times its median employee's."],"src":["SRC-0115","SRC-W0046","SRC-0117","SRC-0118","SRC-0116","SRC-V0157","SRC-V0158"]}]},"FND-00001":{"title":"BlackRock","dek":"The world's largest asset manager, a top owner of the companies arming Israel, and a buyer of its war bonds.","sections":[{"h":"Named by the UN","body":["UN Special Rapporteur Francesca Albanese's July 2025 report names BlackRock the second-largest institutional investor in Palantir (8.6%), Microsoft (7.8%), Amazon (6.6%), Alphabet (6.6%) and IBM (8.6%), and third-largest in Lockheed Martin (7.2%) and Caterpillar (7.5%). It also records BlackRock buying $68M of the Israeli treasury bonds that 'played a critical role in funding the ongoing assault on Gaza'."],"src":["SRC-V0004"]},{"h":"2026 holdings","body":["At 30 June 2026 BlackRock reported 110.5M RTX shares ($21.0B), 61.0M Boeing shares ($13.2B), 18.9M Lockheed Martin shares ($9.6B), 11.8M Northrop Grumman shares ($6.0B) and 6.4% of General Dynamics. Data providers list it at about 9.5% of BAE Systems. Assets under management hit a record $15.3T in Q2 2026."],"src":["SRC-0056","SRC-0064","SRC-0071","SRC-V0151"]},{"h":"Leadership and stance","body":["BlackRock was co-founded in 1988 by Larry Fink, Robert Kapito, Susan Wagner and five others. Fink told CNBC on 13 Oct 2023 that 'we have the largest ETF in Israel' and that nobody was divesting from Israel 'for political reasons'. The Jerusalem Post reports BlackRock 'has also opposed anti-Israel BDS-aligned resolutions'."],"src":["SRC-W0058","SRC-V0162","SRC-W0059"]}]},"PER-00001":{"title":"Larry Fink","dek":"BlackRock's founder and CEO, who saw Israel's military spending as a market story.","sections":[{"h":"Money","body":["BlackRock paid Larry Fink $37.7M for 2025, up from $30.8M for 2024. Forbes puts his net worth at $1.3B. He has chaired the World Economic Forum since August 2025."],"src":["SRC-V0157","SRC-V0159","SRC-V0160"]},{"h":"On the record","body":["On 13 Oct 2023 Fink told CNBC that Israel was 'going to have to be spending a lot more money on military' and that 'we are not seeing anybody looking to divest from Israel for political reasons.' During the 2026 war on Iran he told Fox News: 'Buy more here. This is a good long-term opportunity.' The Jerusalem Post describes his 'deeply personal support for Israel'."],"src":["SRC-V0162","SRC-V0164","SRC-W0059"]},{"h":"Role in the war economy","body":["Fink runs the firm the UN Special Rapporteur names among the largest owners of Palantir, Microsoft, Amazon, Alphabet, Lockheed Martin and Caterpillar, and which bought $68M in Israeli treasury bonds. The UN Commission of Inquiry concluded in September 2025 that Israel committed genocide in Gaza."],"src":["SRC-V0004","SRC-V0025"]}]},"PER-00002":{"title":"Robert S. Kapito","dek":"BlackRock co-founder and president.","sections":[{"h":"Money and role","body":["BlackRock paid Robert Kapito $25.7M for 2025, up from $21.8M in 2024. He co-founded the firm the UN Special Rapporteur names among the largest owners of companies tied to Israel's genocide in Gaza. From 2016 he chaired UJA-Federation of New York, which by December 2023 had raised $156M for its Israel Emergency Fund."],"src":["SRC-V0158","SRC-V0004","SRC-V0168"]},{"h":"Record","body":["Kapito's wealth was put at about $400M in 2022."],"src":["SRC-W0058","SRC-V0166"]}]},"FND-00002":{"title":"Vanguard","dek":"The index giant that is the largest owner of Caterpillar and Palantir and a buyer of Israel's war bonds.","sections":[{"h":"Named by the UN","body":["The UN Special Rapporteur's 2025 report names Vanguard the largest institutional investor in Caterpillar (9.8%), Chevron (8.9%) and Palantir (9.1%), second-largest in Lockheed Martin (9.2%) and Elbit Systems (2.0%), and a buyer of $546M of Israeli treasury bonds, eight times BlackRock's purchase."],"src":["SRC-V0004"]},{"h":"2026 holdings","body":["At 31 March 2026 Vanguard reported 7.47% of RTX (about $19.4B), 7.45% of Boeing ($11.7B), 7.22% of Northrop Grumman, 6.99% of Lockheed Martin and 6.88% of General Dynamics in SEC 13G filings."],"src":["SRC-0060","SRC-0068","SRC-0065","SRC-0055","SRC-0063"]},{"h":"Who runs it","body":["Tim Buckley was replaced as CEO in July 2024 by Salim Ramji, a former BlackRock executive. Vanguard is owned by its funds and manages about $13.3T."],"src":["SRC-V0030"]}]},"PER-N-RAMJI":{"title":"Salim Ramji","dek":"Vanguard CEO since July 2024.","sections":[{"h":"Role","body":["Salim Ramji, a BlackRock veteran, took over Vanguard in July 2024, replacing Tim Buckley. He runs the firm the UN Special Rapporteur names as Palantir's and Caterpillar's largest institutional investor and a $546M buyer of Israeli treasury bonds."],"src":["SRC-V0030","SRC-V0004"]}]},"FND-00003":{"title":"State Street","dek":"The largest owner of Lockheed Martin.","sections":[{"h":"Holdings","body":["State Street held about 14.3% of Lockheed Martin ($16.8B) and 9.27% of Northrop Grumman ($6.7B) at 30 June 2026, plus 4.81% of Boeing ($8.2B). RTX's 2026 proxy lists it at 6.8%. It managed $5.7T at the end of 2025."],"src":["SRC-0057","SRC-0066","SRC-0061","SRC-V0153"]},{"h":"Leadership","body":["Chairman and CEO Ronald O'Hanley worried publicly in May 2026 about the fertilizer shock from the war on Iran, not about the bombs his firm's holdings supply."],"src":["SRC-W0050","SRC-W0051"]}]},"FND-00004":{"title":"Fidelity / FMR","dek":"Johnson family's fund house: 7% of Boeing, and a charity arm that funded the IDF's US backers.","sections":[{"h":"Holdings","body":["FMR reported 7.0% of Boeing at 31 Dec 2025, and at 30 June 2026 9.9M RTX shares ($1.9B), 4.1M General Dynamics shares ($1.5B), 1.7M Lockheed Martin shares and 1.3M Northrop Grumman shares. It manages $7.1T."],"src":["SRC-0070","SRC-0059","SRC-0003"]},{"h":"Charity arm","body":["Rolling Stone reported that Fidelity Charitable, the group's donor-advised fund, blocked giving to UNRWA USA in 2024 while having passed $2.2M to Friends of the IDF, $2.7M to AIPAC's educational affiliate and $19.5M to the Central Fund of Israel, which funds West Bank settlements."],"src":["SRC-V0172"]}]},"PER-00003":{"title":"Abigail Johnson","dek":"Fidelity chair and CEO, worth $41.6B.","sections":[{"h":"Money","body":["Forbes estimates Abigail Johnson owns about 29% of Fidelity, which manages $7.1T, and puts her net worth at $41.6B. She owns about 29% of the company."],"src":["SRC-V0170"]},{"h":"Role","body":["Fidelity held 7.0% of Boeing, maker of the JDAM kits and GBU-39 bombs used in Gaza, at the end of 2025, and its charity arm passed money to Friends of the IDF and settlement funders, according to Rolling Stone."],"src":["SRC-0070","SRC-V0172"]}]},"FND-00005":{"title":"Capital Group","dek":"Largest owner of BAE Systems.","sections":[{"h":"Holdings","body":["Capital Group, founded in 1931 by Jonathan Bell Lovelace and managing $3.6T, held 11.04% of BAE Systems in January 2026, and 5.2% of Boeing, 5.2% of Northrop Grumman and 3.9% of RTX at 31 March 2026. BAE builds the rear fuselage of every F-35."],"src":["SRC-V0155","SRC-0072","SRC-0069","SRC-0067","SRC-0062"]}]},"FND-00006":{"title":"Longview Asset Management","dek":"Crown family office holding about 10% of General Dynamics.","sections":[{"h":"Holding","body":["Filings do: Longview Asset Management filed a Form 3 in 2026 reporting about 10% of General Dynamics, maker of the MK-80 bomb bodies sold to Israel."],"src":["SRC-0074","SRC-0039"]}]},"PER-00012":{"title":"James D. Taiclet","dek":"Lockheed Martin CEO, $70M over three years.","sections":[{"h":"Pay","body":["Lockheed's proxy reports Taiclet's total pay at $22.8M for 2023, $23.8M for 2024 and $23.5M for 2025. He came to the company from American Tower."],"src":["SRC-0116"]},{"h":"Weapons and words","body":["Lockheed builds the F-35 that Israel flies over Gaza; an Israeli F-35 carried out the July 2024 al-Mawasi strike that killed 90 people, according to Campaign Against Arms Trade. Lockheed was principal contractor for the 3,000 Hellfire missiles notified for Israel in February 2025. Taiclet told investors in October 2023 about 'supplemental requests related to support Ukraine, Israel', and in April 2026 called the moment 'a golden opportunity'."],"src":["SRC-V0192","SRC-0029","SRC-V0193","SRC-V0194"]}]},"PER-00013":{"title":"Phebe N. Novakovic","dek":"General Dynamics CEO, the best paid of the arms-maker chiefs.","sections":[{"h":"Pay","body":["General Dynamics' proxy reports Novakovic's total pay at $22.6M (2023), $23.8M (2024) and $25.9M (2025), $72.3M in all."],"src":["SRC-0115"]},{"h":"Bombs","body":["AFSC reports GD's MK-84 2,000-pound bombs 'have been used extensively by the Israeli Air Force during its frequent assaults on Gaza'. GD was a prime contractor on the $2.04B bomb-body sale approved under an emergency declaration in February 2025. In Q1 2026 its backlog rose to $131B."],"src":["SRC-V0195","SRC-0039","SRC-V0198"]}]},"COM-00005":{"title":"Northrop Grumman","dek":"Builds the centre of every F-35 Israel flies.","sections":[{"h":"Role","body":["Northrop Grumman 'designed and produces the center fuselage for all three F-35 variants' and delivered the centre fuselage for Israel's first F-35 in 2015. CEO Kathy Warden was paid $25.4M for 2025, 224 times the median employee."],"src":["SRC-W0048","SRC-W0046"]},{"h":"Owners","body":["State Street held 9.27%, BlackRock 8.29%, Vanguard 7.22% and Capital Group 5.2% of Northrop in 2026."],"src":["SRC-0066","SRC-0056","SRC-0065","SRC-0067"]}]},"PER-NEW-WARDEN":{"title":"Kathy Warden","dek":"Northrop Grumman chair, CEO and president.","sections":[{"h":"Pay and words","body":["Warden's total pay was $25.4M for 2025, up from $24.4M for 2024. In October 2023 she told investors that 'the administration continues to make supplemental requests for urgent needs, including those in Ukraine and Israel.' Her company builds the centre fuselage of Israel's F-35s."],"src":["SRC-W0046","SRC-W0047","SRC-V0193","SRC-W0048"]}]},"PER-00015":{"title":"Robert K. Ortberg","dek":"Boeing CEO since August 2024.","sections":[{"h":"Record","body":["Calhoun was CEO from January 2020 until Kelly Ortberg took over on 8 August 2024."],"src":["SRC-W0061"]},{"h":"Pay and bombs","body":["Ortberg was paid $23.6M for 2025. Boeing was lead contractor in the $6.75B package of bombs and JDAM kits notified for Israel in February 2025, and Sludge counts $26.7B of US arms sales to Israel since October 2023 that benefit Boeing. In April 2026 he said Boeing was 'already seeing higher demand in our military business'."],"src":["SRC-0118","SRC-0027","SRC-V0202","SRC-V0203"]}]},"PER-00014":{"title":"Christopher T. Calio","dek":"RTX chairman and CEO; successor to Greg Hayes.","sections":[{"h":"Record","body":["Calio took over as CEO in May 2024 and Hayes left the board on 30 April 2025."],"src":["SRC-W0049"]},{"h":"Pay and profits","body":["RTX paid Calio $24.8M for 2025 and $55.6M over 2023–2025. In October 2023 Hayes told investors the Israel war meant 'across the entire Raytheon portfolio, you're going to see a benefit of this restocking.' RTX reported a record $289B backlog in July 2026."],"src":["SRC-0117","SRC-V0193","SRC-V0201"]}]},"PER-00006":{"title":"Bernie Marcus","dek":"Home Depot co-founder and long-time funder of Israel's army charity (died 2024).","sections":[{"h":"Giving to the army and lobby","body":["Bernie Marcus gave Friends of the IDF nearly $13M between 2009 and 2022, according to Wikipedia's FIDF entry, and in October 2023 Forbes reported he was supporting Magen David Adom and 'the IDF (the Israel Defense Forces)'. Wikipedia reports he gave $2M to AIPAC in 2023. He co-founded the Israel Democracy Institute and gave $25M toward MDA's national blood centre in 2016."],"src":["SRC-V0184","SRC-W0054","SRC-V0183"]},{"h":"Money","body":["Marcus died on 4 November 2024, when Forbes valued his fortune at $10.3B; by 2019 he had given away $2B."],"src":["SRC-V0183"]}]},"PER-00009":{"title":"Miriam Adelson","dek":"Casino billionaire and Donald Trump's third-largest 2024 donor.","sections":[{"h":"Money and politics","body":["Forbes puts the Adelson family at $31.4B; a 2026 filing shows Miriam Adelson personally holds 52.7% of Las Vegas Sands. She gave $106M to Trump's 2024 campaign and sought his support for annexing the occupied West Bank. Weeks after he took office, his administration used an emergency declaration to approve $2.04B in bomb bodies for Israel."],"src":["SRC-V0189","SRC-0146","SRC-V0021","SRC-0039"]}]},"PER-00007":{"title":"Haim Saban","dek":"Democratic megadonor who calls Israel his one issue.","sections":[{"h":"Money and politics","body":["Forbes puts Haim Saban at $3.3B. He says: 'I'm a one-issue guy, and my issue is Israel.' He was the Democratic Party's top contributor in 2001–2002 and is listed among supporters of Friends of the IDF."],"src":["SRC-V0185","SRC-V0186","SRC-V0184"]}]},"PER-00005":{"title":"Paul Singer","dek":"Elliott Management founder and Republican funder of hawkish pro-Israel groups.","sections":[{"h":"Money and politics","body":["Forbes puts Paul Singer at $7.2B. He gave $5M to Donald Trump's 2024 campaign, sits on the board of the Republican Jewish Coalition, and his foundation reported grants to Friends of the IDF."],"src":["SRC-V0181","SRC-V0182"]}]},"ORG-00013":{"title":"Friends of the IDF (FIDF)","dek":"The tax-deductible US charity for Israel's army; revenue tripled in 2023.","sections":[{"h":"Money","body":["FIDF's revenue rose from $89.3M in 2022 to $282.0M in 2023, and it raised over $50M in the weeks after 7 October 2023. Its biggest named donors include Larry Ellison ($16.6M in 2017; $25M in all per Wikipedia) and Bernie Marcus (nearly $13M, 2009–2022)."],"src":["SRC-V0184","SRC-0048"]}]},"PER-NEW-BLOOMBERG":{"title":"Michael Bloomberg","dek":"Media billionaire who led an $88M gift to Israel's emergency service.","sections":[{"h":"Gifts","body":["In October 2023 Bloomberg matched nearly $44M from about 34,000 donors for an $88M gift to Magen David Adom, the largest in American Friends of MDA's history, paying for ambulances, ballistic vests and helmets. He said: 'America has always been a friend to Israel and I am encouraged that so many of us are stepping up to help our ally.' Bloomberg Philanthropies then committed $27.8M to 65 hard-hit Israeli towns."],"src":["SRC-W0036","SRC-W0037","SRC-W0038"]},{"h":"Money","body":["Forbes puts Bloomberg's net worth at $94.9B (4 Oct 2026), and he owns 88% of Bloomberg LP."],"src":["SRC-W0039"]}]},"PER-NEW-GRAY":{"title":"Jonathan Gray","dek":"Blackstone president; $125M to Tel Aviv University.","sections":[{"h":"Gift","body":["In May 2025 Jonathan Gray and his wife Mindy gave $125M to Tel Aviv University's medical faculty, the largest gift in its history; Gray said the idea grew from discussions after 7 October 2023 and that 'we just wanted to show up and say we support you'."],"src":["SRC-W0040","SRC-W0041"]},{"h":"Money","body":["Gray has been Blackstone's president and COO since 2018; Forbes values him at $9.2B."],"src":["SRC-W0042"]}]},"PER-NEW-SCHWARZMAN":{"title":"Stephen Schwarzman","dek":"Blackstone chair and CEO, Trump backer.","sections":[{"h":"Money and politics","body":["Forbes values Schwarzman at $40.5B. In 2018 he gave $10M to the National Library of Israel for an education centre in his name, his first gift in Israel. In May 2024 he endorsed Donald Trump, saying 'the dramatic rise of antisemitism has led me to focus on the consequences of upcoming elections with greater urgency.'"],"src":["SRC-W0045","SRC-W0043","SRC-W0044"]}]},"PER-N-NETANYAHU":{"title":"Benjamin Netanyahu: the case file","dek":"Thirty years of crying bomb, three wars, one arrest warrant, one genocide-incitement finding, one corruption trial, and the US money and arms that paid for all of it.","sections":[{"h":"Benjamin Netanyahu","intro":true,"body":["Benjamin \"Bibi\" Netanyahu, also known as \"Satanyahu,\" is the ninth and longest-serving prime minister of the imaginary state of Israel, i.e. occupied Palestine. Bibi is one of the chief architects of Israel's apartheid regime, the genocide in Gaza, the ongoing annexation of the West Bank and the campaign of annihilation against South Lebanon. Since October 2023, he has directed Israel's wars on Gaza and Lebanon and helped drag the region into war with Iran, a country he has been publicly threatening since at least the 1990s. In November 2024, the ICC issued an arrest warrant for him over alleged war crimes and crimes against humanity in Gaza, including starvation as a method of warfare, murder and persecution. In September 2025, a UN Commission of Inquiry concluded that Israel had committed genocide in Gaza and found that Netanyahu, Isaac Herzog and Yoav Gallant had incited it. He ordered Israel's invasion of Lebanon in October 2024, presided over the June 2025 strikes that began the Twelve-Day War with Iran, and spent the following months lobbying Donald Trump for an even bigger one. When he is not bombing neighbouring countries, Bibi is also a criminal defendant at home: he has been under indictment for bribery, fraud and breach of trust since 2019. Somewhere along the way, billionaire Oracle founder Larry Ellison reportedly offered him a seat on Oracle's board worth roughly half a million dollars per year."],"src":["SRC-V0019","SRC-V0025","SRC-V0026","SRC-V0020","SRC-V0027"]},{"h":"Lebanon: on his watch, on his orders","body":["He was prime minister for every month of it. His government sent the army into South Lebanon on 1 October 2024, and a week later he went on camera to warn the Lebanese of 'destruction and suffering like we see in Gaza'. He delivered it. By the November 2024 ceasefire about 1.2 million people had been driven from their homes and more than 4,000 were dead.","Then he refused to leave. Israel ignored the withdrawal deadline and kept five hilltops, and in March 2026 he turned them into an occupation zone: 'I have just instructed to further expand the existing security buffer zone,' he said on 29 March. By the summer it covered 570 to 700 km². On 26 June he said what it is for: 'We are not allowing Hezbollah, nor the Lebanese population to enter that area.' His military minister, Israel Katz, boasted of razing 24 villages, 'not house by house, but entire villages'. UN experts said the destruction 'points to ethnic cleansing' of the Shia south.","The toll under his government: 8,953 killed and 30,643 wounded by 17 September 2026, according to Lebanon's health ministry. The full record is in the South Lebanon case file."],"src":["SRC-W0169","SRC-W0149","SRC-W0167","SRC-W0153","SRC-W0163","SRC-W0147","SRC-W0154","SRC-W0165"]},{"h":"Iran: four decades of \"soon\"","body":["Then there is Iran, Netanyahu's favourite apocalypse, now in roughly its fourth decade of coming \"soon\". In 1992 he told the Knesset that \"within three to five years\" Iran could build a bomb, and his 1995 book Fighting Terrorism repeated the forecast. In 2002 he went before a US congressional committee to push for the invasion of Iraq and warn of an Iraqi and Iranian race for the bomb.","In September 2012 he stood at the UN General Assembly holding a cartoon bomb with a red line drawn on it and predicted: \"By next spring, at most by next summer.\" For decades he stood before cameras, Congresses and diagrams explaining that Iran was always somewhere between \"almost there\" and \"we must attack immediately\".","Thirty years of countdown clocks later, in the early hours of 13 June 2025, Israel launched the strikes that opened the Twelve-Day War. The United States bombed Fordow, Natanz and Isfahan on 22 June. Iran's death toll is estimated at between 1,060 and 1,190. In early 2026 Netanyahu lobbied Donald Trump for a joint strike targeting Iran's leadership, and on 28 February 2026 the United States and Israel went to war with Iran again."],"src":["SRC-W0050","SRC-W0051","SRC-W0052","SRC-V0020"]},{"h":"The warrant","body":["On 21 November 2024 the International Criminal Court issued arrest warrants for Netanyahu and his former military minister Yoav Gallant. The judges found reasonable grounds to believe that, from at least 8 October 2023 until at least 20 May 2024, both men bore criminal responsibility for the war crime of starvation as a method of warfare and for the crimes against humanity of murder, persecution and other inhumane acts.","The response was to attack the court. In February 2025 the Trump administration imposed asset freezes and travel bans on ICC personnel. During Netanyahu's April 2025 visit to Budapest, Viktor Orbán announced that Hungary would leave the court."],"src":["SRC-W0018","SRC-V0019"]},{"h":"The incitement finding","body":["On 16 September 2025 the UN Independent International Commission of Inquiry concluded that Israeli authorities and security forces had committed four of the five genocidal acts listed in the 1948 Genocide Convention.","It also found that President Isaac Herzog, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu and then-military minister Yoav Gallant \"have incited the commission of genocide\", and that Israeli authorities had failed to punish them."],"src":["SRC-V0025"]},{"h":"The defendant","body":["Netanyahu has been under indictment since 21 November 2019 for bribery, fraud and breach of trust, and his trial opened on 24 May 2020. Case 1000 alleges that he and his wife received cigars, champagne and jewellery worth about $195,000 from the businessmen Arnon Milchan and James Packer. Case 2000 alleges that he discussed favourable coverage in Yedioth Ahronoth with its publisher Arnon Mozes in exchange for legislation to hobble the rival paper Israel Hayom. Case 4000, the bribery count, alleges that he granted regulatory favours to Bezeq's owner Shaul Elovitch in return for flattering coverage on Elovitch's site Walla.","He began testifying in December 2024. On 30 November 2025 he asked President Herzog for a pardon, after a letter from Donald Trump urging clemency. In April 2026 Herzog deferred the request and pushed for a plea deal."],"src":["SRC-W0053","SRC-W0028"]},{"h":"The Oracle seat","body":["In September 2021 Haaretz reported that Larry Ellison, the billionaire founder of Oracle, had offered Netanyahu a seat on Oracle's board. The offer was estimated at some $450,000, and Netanyahu would have had to leave the Knesset to take it. Netanyahu denied the report.","The month before, he had holidayed on Ellison's Hawaiian island of Lanai, even though Ellison is one of several hundred prosecution witnesses in his corruption trial."],"src":["SRC-W0054","SRC-V0027","SRC-W0053"]},{"h":"The paymaster","body":["Netanyahu negotiated and celebrated the 2016 memorandum of understanding: $38B over ten years, about $3.8B a year, mostly spent on US-made arms. He called it \"the largest military aid package the US has ever given out to any nation\". When Congress passed $17B in military aid for Israel in April 2024, he posted: \"Thank you friends, thank you America!\" Two months later he publicly accused Washington of withholding weapons and demanded: \"give us the tools and we'll finish the job a lot faster\". Brown University's Costs of War Project puts US military aid to Israel from October 2023 to September 2025 at $21.7B.","At home, in December 2025 he announced NIS 350B (about $110B) over ten years to build Israel's own arms industry, much of which is state-owned Rafael and IAI. In March 2026, with the Iran war under way, his cabinet added NIS 32B ($10.4B) to the military budget. The Knesset then passed a record NIS 142B for the Israel Ministry of Defense. The Bank of Israel puts the fiscal cost of the wars from 2023 to 2026 at about NIS 350B, roughly half of it borrowed. As prime minister he draws about NIS 59,000 a month."],"src":["SRC-W0041","SRC-W0043","SRC-W0044","SRC-0085","SRC-W0045","SRC-W0046","SRC-W0014","SRC-W0015","SRC-W0007"]}],"quotes":[{"text":"Within three to five years, we can assume that Iran will become autonomous in its ability to develop and produce a nuclear bomb.","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, Knesset, 1992","src":"SRC-W0050"},{"text":"By next spring, at most by next summer … they will have finished the medium enrichment and move on to the final stage.","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, UN General Assembly, 2012","src":"SRC-W0050"},{"text":"We have operated with impressive success in thwarting Iran from establishing a military foothold in Syria, in the framework of which the military has struck hundreds of times against Iranian and Hezbollah targets.","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, cabinet meeting, 13 January 2019","src":"SRC-W0048"},{"text":"give us the tools and we'll finish the job a lot faster","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, video, 18 June 2024","src":"SRC-W0044"},{"text":"Thank you friends, thank you America!","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, on the April 2024 US aid package","src":"SRC-W0043"},{"text":"I approved a total of 350 billion shekels over the coming decade to build an independent arms industry for the State of Israel","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, 25 December 2025","src":"SRC-W0045"},{"text":"ordered a complete siege on the Gaza Strip. There will be no electricity, no food, no water, no fuel. Everything is closed. We are fighting human animals and we are acting accordingly","who":"Yoav Gallant, 9 October 2023","src":"SRC-W0017"},{"text":"It's an entire nation out there that is responsible. It is not true, this rhetoric about civilians who were not aware or not involved.","who":"Isaac Herzog, October 2023","src":"SRC-W0026"},{"text":"Nobody will let us cause 2 million civilians to die of hunger even though it might be justified and moral until our hostages are returned.","who":"Bezalel Smotrich, 5 August 2024","src":"SRC-W0010"},{"text":"If Khamenei continues to fire missiles at the Israeli home front, Tehran will burn.","who":"Israel Katz, 14 June 2025","src":"SRC-W0022"},{"text":"The only thing that should enter Gaza as long as Hamas doesn't release the hostages it holds is not a gram of humanitarian aid, but hundreds of tons of explosives from the Air Force.","who":"Itamar Ben Gvir, 17 October 2023","src":"SRC-W0003"}]},"case12":{"title":"Global: the genocide's suppliers beyond the West","dek":"Oil from Baku and Astana, coal from Richards Bay, drones and shells from India, gas money from Cairo: the countries outside Europe and the US that kept Israel's machine fed since October 2023, and the few that cut it off.","sections":[{"h":"The genocide's global supply chain","body":["Europe and the United States supply the bombs, but the Gaza genocide also runs on oil from the Caspian, coal from South Africa, drones and artillery rounds from India, and gas money from Egypt. Twenty-five countries delivered 323 shipments of crude and refined fuel, 21.2 million tonnes in all, to Israel between November 2023 and October 2025, according to Oil Change International.","Israel was the world's seventh-largest arms exporter in 2021–25, per SIPRI, and its customers in Asia, Africa and Latin America pay for the 'combat-proven' label earned in Gaza, Lebanon and Iran."],"src":["SRC-W0067","SRC-V0034"]},{"h":"India: drones, shells, workers and a Knesset medal","body":["India took 29% of Israel's arms exports in 2021–25 (SIPRI). Adani-Elbit's Hyderabad plant delivered more than 20 Hermes 900 drones to Israel in early 2024. Amnesty counts at least 2,596 arms shipments from India to Israel between October 2023 and November 2025, including 1,000 155-mm high-explosive rounds from state-owned Munitions India to Elbit. Premier Explosives credited an 'Israel export order' for an 'exponential jump' in revenue.","Around 16,000 Indian workers replaced barred Palestinian labourers in 2024. Prime Minister Narendra Modi addressed the Knesset in February 2026 ('India stands with Israel firmly') and signed 16 agreements with Benjamin Netanyahu. India's Supreme Court refused to intervene, calling arms exports foreign policy."],"src":["SRC-W0057","SRC-W0058","SRC-W0061","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0060","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0059","SRC-W0062","SRC-V0034"]},{"h":"Oil: Azerbaijan, Kazakhstan, Turkey, Brazil","body":["Azerbaijan supplied over 7 million tonnes (nearly 40%), and Kazakhstan around 5.3 million tonnes, of the crude and fuel shipped to Israel from November 2023 to October 2025. Azeri crude flows through the BP-operated BTC pipeline and loads at Ceyhan, where Turkey collects $1.27 a barrel despite its May 2024 trade ban. Kazakh crude comes largely from Chevron- and ExxonMobil-owned Tengizchevroil. Brazil supplied about 9% of Israel's crude before direct shipments halted in 2025."],"src":["SRC-W0068","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0073","SRC-W0072"]},{"h":"Coal: Colombia stopped, South Africa filled the gap","body":["Colombian coal was 60% of Israel's coal imports in 2023–24, per the UN Special Rapporteur report. Petro banned it in August 2024; the next government reversed the ban in August 2026. South Africa, the applicant in the ICJ genocide case, shipped at least 2.99 million tonnes from Richards Bay from October 2023 to December 2025, and Glencore is a primary supplier from both countries."],"src":["SRC-W0086","SRC-W0084","SRC-W0088"]},{"h":"Egypt, Morocco and the normalisers","body":["Egypt signed a $35B, 130 bcm deal for Leviathan gas through 2040 in August 2025. At Rafah, a Sisi-linked firm charged fleeing Palestinians $5,000 per adult. Morocco bought 36 Elbit ATMOS howitzers and, per Military Africa, a $500M Barak MX system, and its ports handled ships carrying F-35 parts. Kazakhstan joined the Abraham Accords in November 2025. Argentina's Milei signed military MoUs and promised a Jerusalem embassy, and Paraguay reopened its own there in December 2024."],"src":["SRC-W0071","SRC-W0091","SRC-W0078","SRC-W0081","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0093"]},{"h":"Asia and Africa: customers of the laboratory","body":["Vietnam signed for about $250M of Rafael Spike missiles in 2026 and about $700M of IAI satellites. The Philippines paid Elbit P248M for guided shells in August 2025 before freezing new deals. Singapore chose the Hermes 900. South Korean arms exports to Israel continued, and Japan tested Israeli attack drones. Rwanda, Nigeria, Kenya and Cameroon appear as clients of Israeli spyware, riot gear and rifles.","Contrasts: Colombia, Chad and Brazil recalled ambassadors or cut ties; Japan's Itochu ended its Elbit partnership, citing the ICJ."],"src":["SRC-W0105","SRC-W0102","SRC-W0106","SRC-W0098","SRC-W0103","SRC-W0083","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0104","SRC-W0099","SRC-W0094","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0095","SRC-W0097","SRC-W0100"]}]},"caseSL":{"title":"Erased: the destruction of South Lebanon","dek":"Villages flattened, a fifth of the country driven out, nearly 9,000 dead, and a 700 km² zone the people who lived there may not enter. October 2023 to 2026.","sections":[{"h":"Erased from the map: the flattening of South Lebanon's villages","body":["Israel did not merely bomb South Lebanon; it set out to unmake it. By 5 November 2024, five weeks into the ground invasion launched on 1 October 2024, Lebanon's National News Agency counted more than 40,000 housing units completely destroyed across 37 devastated southern towns, while Information International researcher Mohamed Chamseddine counted at least 29 villages completely destroyed (L'Orient Today). Amnesty International found that between 1 October 2024 and 26 January 2025 more than 10,000 structures across 24 municipalities were heavily damaged or destroyed, much of it by controlled demolition carried out after Israeli troops had secured the ground, 'outside of combat action'. In Dhayra, 71 per cent of buildings were gone. Amnesty said the destruction 'must be investigated as war crimes'. The UN human rights office puts the 16 September to 27 November 2024 toll at nearly 64,000 buildings destroyed or damaged.","The 2026 offensive finished the job. On 31 March 2026 Military minister Israel Katz announced that 'all homes in Lebanese villages near the border will be destroyed — in accordance with the Rafah and Biet Hanoun model in Gaza' (Times of Israel). Bellingcat's satellite review found that by 8 May 2026 at least 46 of the 54 towns and villages inside Israel's 'Yellow Line' had been 'heavily damaged or, in some cases, entirely flattened'. Lighthouse Reports and the Financial Times found almost 85,000 housing units damaged or destroyed in the south, and that 65 per cent of destruction indicators came after the 16 April 2026 ceasefire. Lebanon's CNRS counted more than 50,424 residential units damaged or destroyed between 2 March and 16 April alone.","Then Katz confessed it. On 29 July 2026 he boasted: 'Twenty-four Lebanese villages, hundreds of years old—we destroyed all the buildings, not house by house, but entire villages... 15,000 to 20,000 homes across all 24 villages have been destroyed' (Times of Israel). On the same reporting he said of the residents: 'They have nowhere to return to; everything there has been destroyed' (L'Orient Today, 3 August 2026)."],"src":["SRC-W0140","SRC-W0141","SRC-W0142","SRC-W0143","SRC-W0144","SRC-W0145","SRC-W0146","SRC-W0147","SRC-W0148"]},{"h":"One in five uprooted: a population driven out, and kept out","body":["Twice in two years Israel has driven roughly a fifth of Lebanon from its homes. On 4 October 2024 Prime Minister Najib Mikati put the number at 1.2 million people, out of a 2022 population of 5.49 million (ABC News). The IOM's Displacement Tracking Matrix, which counts only those it can register, recorded 899,725 displaced as of 25 November 2024, the eve of the ceasefire (OCHA). A year later, in November 2025, the UN said more than 64,000 people, mainly from the south, were still displaced (Times of Israel, citing the UN).","From 2 March 2026 the expulsion was repeated at greater speed. By 16 March, 1,049,328 people had self-registered as displaced (WHO). The UN put the figure at more than 1.2 million by 29 March (Al Jazeera). Israeli orders told every resident south of the Litani, then south of the Zahrani river roughly 40 km from the Blue Line, to leave (OHCHR). UN experts said the displaced amounted to 'approximately one in five of Lebanon's population'.","The displacement has not ended. The UN Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination reported that more than 360,000 people remained displaced at the end of July 2026; Al Jazeera, citing Lebanese figures, reported 360,000 still displaced in August, at least 22,000 of them in collective shelters. On 29 September 2026 The New Humanitarian, citing the UN, reported that more than 864,000 had returned but more than 341,000 remain displaced. Many of those who 'returned' came back to rubble; the ones who cannot return come from the zone Israel still holds."],"src":["SRC-W0149","SRC-W0150","SRC-W0151","SRC-W0152","SRC-W0153","SRC-W0142","SRC-W0154","SRC-W0155","SRC-W0156","SRC-W0157"]},{"h":"Occupation by another name: from five hilltops to a 700 km² 'security zone'","body":["The 27 November 2024 ceasefire gave Israel 60 days to withdraw. It did not. On 26 January 2025, the withdrawal deadline, residents who tried to walk back to their villages were met with gunfire: Israeli forces killed at least 22 people, including two women and a Lebanese soldier, and injured more than 124 (CBS News). After a second deadline on 18 February 2025 Israel kept five hilltops inside Lebanon: al-Aziyah, al-Awaida, el-Hamames, Jabal Bilat and Labbouneh (Al Jazeera). By the ceasefire's first anniversary UNIFIL had recorded more than 10,000 Israeli air and ground violations (Anadolu, citing UNIFIL); the UN counted at least 127 civilians killed since the truce and a wall crossing into Lebanese territory that made 4,000 square metres inaccessible (Times of Israel, citing the UN).","In March 2026 Netanyahu turned the five points into a zone. Israel's ground invasion began on 16 March. On 29 March Netanyahu said: 'I have just instructed to further expand the existing security buffer zone' (Al Jazeera). By June Israel held about 570 km² (Al Jazeera); in July Katz claimed '700 square kilometers in southern Lebanon — from the coastline until the Beaufort Castle and the foothills of the Hermon' (Times of Israel). The New Humanitarian reports Israel occupies around 68 towns and villages.","Netanyahu has stated the purpose plainly. On 26 June 2026 he said the zone would be held 'until Hezbollah disarms' and added: 'We are not allowing Hezbollah, nor the Lebanese population to enter that area' (Times of Israel). The killing goes on: strikes killed at least 11 people, including three children, on 15 August 2026 (Times of Israel), and the Health Ministry's count kept rising through 17 September 2026 'as Israeli attacks continue across several areas of southern Lebanon' (Palestinian Information Center)."],"src":["SRC-W0158","SRC-W0159","SRC-W0160","SRC-W0151","SRC-W0161","SRC-W0153","SRC-W0162","SRC-W0147","SRC-W0157","SRC-W0163","SRC-W0164","SRC-W0165"]},{"h":"The dead: nearly 9,000 killed, families buried in their homes","body":["Lebanon's Ministry of Public Health counts 8,953 people killed and 30,643 wounded by Israeli attacks between 8 October 2023 and 17 September 2026. Of these, 4,047 were killed by 4 December 2024, among them 316 children and 790 women; 4,386 were killed between 2 March and 17 September 2026 alone. UNICEF reports at least 261 children killed and 1,056 injured since 2 March 2026, 'an average of seven children killed or injured every day over six months'.","Netanyahu warned the Lebanese on 8 October 2024 of 'destruction and suffering like we see in Gaza' (Al Jazeera). The ministry had already recorded 558 people killed on 23 September 2024, including 50 children and 94 women, Lebanon's deadliest day since its civil war (HRW). The pager and walkie-talkie explosions of 17-18 September 2024 killed at least 37 people, including a nine-year-old girl and an 11-year-old boy, and injured more than 2,931; Amnesty said they 'should be investigated as war crimes'. On 29 September 2024 a strike on a building in Ain el-Delb near Saida likely killed at least 60 civilians, including at least 25 women and 19 children (Airwars). On 14 October 2024 a strike in the Christian village of Aitou killed 23 people displaced from the south, 12 of them women and two of them children (Amnesty; OHCHR). In Baalbek on 21 October 2024 a strike killed six members of the Othman family, two women and four children (Amnesty).","In 2026 the massacres resumed at scale. On 8 April 2026, hours after a ceasefire was announced, Israeli forces struck more than 150 locations in a 10-minute onslaught, killing at least 303 people and wounding 1,150 (UN experts)."],"src":["SRC-W0165","SRC-W0166","SRC-W0167","SRC-W0168","SRC-W0169","SRC-W0181","SRC-W0170","SRC-W0171","SRC-W0172","SRC-W0173","SRC-W0154"]},{"h":"Emptying the Shia south: the evidence behind the charge of ethnic cleansing","body":["Israeli officials have named the community they are removing. On 16 March 2026 Katz said: 'Hundreds of thousands of Shiite residents of south Lebanon who have evacuated...will not return to the area south of the Litani [River] until the safety of the residents of the north is guaranteed' (Times of Israel). Human Rights Watch noted that Israeli evacuation orders cover 'large swaths of the predominantly Shia population in southern Lebanon and the southern suburbs of the capital, Beirut', about 15 per cent of Lebanese territory, and called the displacement a possible war crime. Drop Site News reported that Israeli officers phoned the heads of majority-Christian villages and ordered them to force out the displaced sheltering there; the New York Times reported Israel urging Christian and Druze communities to push Shia residents out (via Democracy Now!).","UN bodies have reached the same conclusion. On 15 April 2026 UN Special Rapporteurs wrote that the deliberate destruction of homes is 'a form of collective punishment, particularly in Shiite areas in the rural South of the country. It also points to ethnic cleansing.' OHCHR found that Israeli leaders 'repeatedly referred to southern Lebanon as \"another Gaza\"' and that the orders 'raise serious concerns of forced displacement, prohibited under international humanitarian law'. On 26 August 2026 the UN Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination cited Katz 'characterising \"the Shia inhabitants of 24 villages in southern Lebanon as enemies\"'.","BL00D.MONEY's charge: mass expulsion of a population identified by sect, the razing of its villages, and a declared ban on its return are the elements of ethnic cleansing, and that is what UN experts say the evidence points to."],"src":["SRC-W0161","SRC-W0174","SRC-W0175","SRC-W0176","SRC-W0154","SRC-W0142","SRC-W0155"]},{"h":"Killing the witnesses and the rescuers, wrecking the means of life","body":["Israel has killed journalists in Lebanon from the first week. On 13 October 2023 an Israeli tank fired two rounds 37 seconds apart at a group of reporters, killing Reuters' Issam Abdallah (Reuters, via NPR); HRW called it an apparent war crime. On 21 November 2023 an Israeli strike killed Al Mayadeen's Farah Omar and Rabih Me'mari at Tayr Harfa (Al Jazeera). On 25 October 2024 a US-made JDAM-guided bomb killed Ghassan Najjar, Mohammad Reda and Wissam Kassem at a Hasbaya compound housing more than a dozen journalists, 'most likely a deliberate attack on civilians' (HRW). In 2026 Israel killed Ali Shoaib and Fatima and Mohamad Ftouni on 28 March (CPJ), and on 22 April it struck Amal Khalil's shelter while paramedics were driven off with a stun grenade (HRW). CPJ counts 16 Lebanese journalists killed in Lebanon as of 24 September 2026.","Rescuers were also killed. OCHA recorded 241 health workers killed between October 2023 and November 2024. The Health Ministry counted 67 attacks on hospitals, 40 of them direct, and 256 emergency vehicles targeted. Since 2 March 2026 it has recorded 179 attacks on ambulance crews and 135 healthcare workers killed (Al Jazeera).","Farmland and water have been destroyed too. HRW verified white phosphorus in at least 17 municipalities by June 2024. Lighthouse/FT found herbicide sprayed over 2,000 hectares and at least 2,500 km of water infrastructure destroyed. The World Bank put 2024 reconstruction needs at US$11 billion against US$14 billion in damage and losses; Lebanon's government estimates 2026 direct damage at US$3-4 billion, and Prime Minister Nawaf Salam says the total may exceed US$27 billion."],"src":["SRC-W0177","SRC-W0178","SRC-V0100","SRC-W0191","SRC-W0179","SRC-W0180","SRC-W0150","SRC-W0167","SRC-W0156","SRC-W0181","SRC-W0145","SRC-W0182","SRC-W0183","SRC-W0184"]}],"quotes":[{"text":"You have an opportunity to save Lebanon before it falls into the abyss of a long war that will lead to destruction and suffering like we see in Gaza","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, video message to the Lebanese people, 8 October 2024","src":"SRC-W0169"},{"text":"I have just instructed to further expand the existing security buffer zone. We are determined to fundamentally change the situation in the north [of Israel].","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, statement, 29 March 2026","src":"SRC-W0153"},{"text":"We are not allowing Hezbollah, nor the Lebanese population to enter that area.","who":"Benjamin Netanyahu, on the south Lebanon buffer zone, 26 June 2026","src":"SRC-W0163"}],"lead":{"text":"Twenty-four Lebanese villages, hundreds of years old—we destroyed all the buildings, not house by house, but entire villages.","who":"Israel Katz, Israeli Military minister in Netanyahu's government, Channel 14 conference, 29 July 2026","src":"SRC-W0147"}}},"arsenal":{"weapons":[{"id":"WPN-00001","name":"JDAM guidance kit family","aka":["Joint Direct Attack Munition","GBU-31/32/38 kits","KMU-556/557/559/572"],"class":"guided bomb","form":"bomb","dims":{"length_m":3.85,"diameter_m":0.46,"span_m":null,"weight_kg":925},"specs":[{"k":"What it is","v":"GPS/INS tail kit that turns unguided Mk-80 bombs and BLU-109 penetrators into guided bombs (GBU-31 = 2,000-lb class)","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Weight (GBU-31 configuration)","v":"2,040 / 2,120 lb (925 / 961 kg) with Mk-84 or BLU-109 body","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Guidance","v":"GPS-aided inertial navigation","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Accuracy (CEP)","v":"5 m with GPS; 30 m on INS only","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Range","v":"up to 24 km (13 nmi)","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Unit cost","v":"$21,000-$36,000 per kit (budget-category range cited by Wikipedia)","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Production contract","v":"$7.48B Boeing JDAM contract, US Air Force, May 2024 (includes foreign military sales)","src":["SRC-0026"]},{"k":"Users","v":"Israel became the first international customer in 2000","src":["SRC-V0251"]}],"makers":[{"entity":"COM-00003","role":"prime / tail kit","src":["SRC-0026"]},{"entity":"Woodward, Inc. (CAGE 81873)","role":"actuation system (fin actuator) components","src":["SRC-V0100"]},{"entity":"AeroAntenna Technology (CAGE 0UVG2)","role":"component of precision guidance package (found on an MPR-500 in Rafah)","src":["SRC-W0099"]}],"parts":[{"name":"Bomb body (Mk-80 series or BLU-109)","maker":"COM-00004 / COM-00022","start":0,"end":0.72,"note":"The kit has no warhead of its own; it guides whatever body it is bolted to. Mk-80 series bodies (Mk-82/83/84) come from General Dynamics' Garland, Texas plant, now owned by REPKON USA; the maker of Israel-bound BLU-109 cases is not established in sources reviewed.","src":["SRC-W0062","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0073","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0078","SRC-W0079","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0081","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0083","SRC-W0084","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0106","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0098","SRC-W0107","SRC-W0094","SRC-W0097","SRC-W0087","SRC-W0100","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0088"]},{"name":"Strake/nose kit","maker":"COM-00003","start":0.3,"end":0.5,"note":"Mid-body strakes supplied with the kit for stability.","src":["SRC-W0062","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0073","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0078","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0073","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0079","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0081","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0083","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0073","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0084","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069"]},{"name":"Tail kit (GPS/INS guidance, control fins)","maker":"COM-00003","start":0.72,"end":1,"note":"Boeing CAGE code 76301 identified on a tail fin at Msayleh, Lebanon (Oct 2025); Boeing tail unit at Deir Seryan (Aug 2025).","src":["SRC-W0062","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0062","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0078","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0087","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0088","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0089","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0089","SRC-W0088","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0091","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0093","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0094","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0094","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0078","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0095","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0063"]},{"name":"Fin actuation system","maker":"Woodward, Inc.","start":0.8,"end":0.95,"note":"HRW identified a Hasbaya fragment with CAGE 81873 as part of the actuation system that moves the fins.","src":["SRC-W0062","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0062","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0078","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0087","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0097","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0089","SRC-W0089","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0088","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0091","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0073","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0098","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0065"]}],"summary":"The JDAM is a Boeing-built tail kit that bolts satellite and inertial guidance onto unguided bombs, so a one-ton bomb can be aimed at a single building. Israel was its first foreign customer in 2000, and in May 2024 the US Air Force gave Boeing a $7.48B JDAM contract that includes foreign sales. On 7 February 2025 the US notified Israel of 13,000 KMU-556 kits for Mk-84 bombs, 3,475 KMU-557 kits for BLU-109 penetrators and 1,004 KMU-572 kits, approved maximums rather than confirmed deliveries. JDAM remnants run through the record of the Gaza genocide and the destruction of South Lebanon: Amnesty traced Boeing kit parts to the al-Najjar and Abu Mu'eileq homes in Deir al-Balah (43 civilians killed, Oct 2023), HRW found US JDAM kits at Younine, Hasbaya and Habbarieh, and Boeing tail units among destroyed reconstruction machinery at Deir Seryan and Msayleh in 2025.","transfers":[{"date":"2025-02-07","what":"13,000 KMU-556 (Mk-84), 3,475 KMU-557 (BLU-109) and 1,004 KMU-572 JDAM kits notified, part of a $6.75B package; approved maximums, deliveries undisclosed","value_usd":6750000000,"src":"https://www.dsca.mil/Press-Media/Major-Arms-Sales/Article-Display/Article/4060920/israel-munitions-guidance-kits-fuzes-and-munitions-support"},{"date":"2025-02-28","what":"1,500 KMU-559C/B and 3,500 KMU-559J/B JDAM kits with 201 Mk-83 and 4,799 BLU-110 bombs; $675.7M package, deliveries estimated from 2028","value_usd":675700000,"src":"https://www.dsca.mil/Press-Media/Major-Arms-Sales/Article-Display/Article/4088252/israel-munitions-guidance-kits-and-munitions-support"}],"status":"documented"},{"id":"WPN-00002","name":"GBU-31 (Mk-84 + KMU-556 JDAM)","aka":["KMU-556 JDAM kit","GBU-31(V)1","2,000-lb JDAM"],"class":"guided bomb","form":"bomb","dims":{"length_m":3.85,"diameter_m":0.46,"span_m":null,"weight_kg":925},"specs":[{"k":"Configuration","v":"Mk-84 2,000-lb bomb body + KMU-556 JDAM tail kit","src":["SRC-0027"]},{"k":"Weight","v":"2,040 lb (925 kg)","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Explosive fill","v":"946 lb (429 kg) of Tritonal, H6 or PBXN-109 (Mk-84 body)","src":["SRC-W0109"]},{"k":"Effects","v":"crater up to 15 m wide, 11 m deep; lethal fragmentation to 120 m (Mk-84)","src":["SRC-W0109"]},{"k":"Accuracy (CEP)","v":"5 m GPS / 30 m INS","src":["SRC-V0251"]},{"k":"Fuze","v":"FMU-152A/B Joint Programmable Fuze (17,475 in the Feb 2025 sale)","src":["SRC-0027"]},{"k":"Unit cost","v":"Mk-84 body ~$16,000 + JDAM kit $21,000-$36,000","src":["SRC-W0109"]}],"makers":[{"entity":"COM-00003","role":"tail kit (KMU-556), principal contractor in Feb 2025 sale","src":["SRC-0027"]},{"entity":"COM-00004","role":"body (GD-OTS Garland, TX named principal contractor for Mk-84/BLU-117 bodies, Feb 2025)","src":["SRC-W0110"]},{"entity":"COM-00022","role":"body (bought GD-OTS Garland operations, April 2025)","src":["SRC-0040"]},{"entity":"McAlester Army Ammunition Plant","role":"principal contractor in Feb 2025 bomb packages (US Army plant)","src":["SRC-W0110"]},{"entity":"COM-00006","role":"explosive fill: operates Holston Army Ammunition Plant, sole RDX/HMX source producing fills 'for every type of ordnance' used by the DoD (no Israel-specific lot traced)","src":["SRC-W0111"]},{"entity":"COM-00020","role":"fuze (FMU-152 Joint Programmable Fuze; now Arcline-owned)","src":["SRC-0030"]},{"entity":"L3Harris Fuzing and Ordnance Systems","role":"fuze, principal contractor in Feb 2025 package","src":["SRC-0027"]}],"parts":[{"name":"Mk-84 bomb body","maker":"COM-00004 / COM-00022","start":0,"end":0.72,"note":"Steel case; Garland, TX plant now owned by REPKON USA.","src":["SRC-W0062","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0093","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0087","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0088","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0087","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0100","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0092","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0063"]},{"name":"Explosive fill (Tritonal / PBXN-109)","maker":"COM-00006 (Holston AAP, RDX-based fills)","start":0.12,"end":0.65,"note":"946 lb fill; Holston is the RDX/HMX source for DoD ordnance, but no source ties a specific Israel-bound bomb to it.","src":["SRC-W0062","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0066","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0094","SRC-W0070","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0089","SRC-W0089","SRC-W0097","SRC-W0086","SRC-W0101","SRC-W0102","SRC-W0067","SRC-W0062","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0096","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0072","SRC-W0103","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0093","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0082","SRC-W0085","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0077","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0063","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0075","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0074","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0069","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0071","SRC-W0080","SRC-W0068","SRC-W0104","SRC-W0064","SRC-W0090","SRC-W0076","SRC-W0065","SRC-W0072","SRC