y owned: 54,979,044.31; Percent of class: 7.0 %","pg":"Items 4(a)-(b)"}],"shares":54979044,"basis":"Schedule 13G/A, FMR LLC"},{"id":"OWN-0026","t":"ownership","a":"FND-00002","b":"COM-00006","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":2345011496,"cur":"GBP","pct":"4.31","date":"2026-06-01","st":"reported","ctx":"Vanguard Capital Management held about 122.1M BAE Systems shares (4.31%) as of 1 June 2026, per a data provider's shareholder list. At that day's closing price of 1,921p, the stake was worth about £2.35B (about $3.2B). Vanguard's Total International Stock ETF alone held 1.52% (43,134,020 shares) on 30 June 2026.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0071","ex":"Vanguard Capital Management, LLC | 4.31% | 122,072,436 | Jun 01, 2026","pg":"Top Institutional Holders table"},{"s":"SRC-V0328","ex":"Jun 1, 2026 | 2,012.00 | 2,020.00 | 1,911.50 | 1,921.00 | 1,921.00 | 4,980,862 (Date, Open, High, Low, Close, Adj Close, Volume; currency GBp)","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0329","ex":"1 GBP = 1.34559 USD","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0330","ex":"Jun 1, 2026 | 1.3450 | 1.3476 | 1.3409 | 1.3452 | 1.3452","pg":null}],"shares":122072436,"basis":"Data provider shareholder list (Investing.com); no UK holding notice required below 5%","amt_usd (new field or ctx)":3155424019},{"id":"OWN-0027","t":"ownership","a":"FND-00001","b":"COM-00006","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":"9.52","date":"2026-07-31","st":"reported","ctx":"BlackRock is among the largest institutional holders of BAE Systems plc. A data provider lists BlackRock, Inc. at about 9.5% of BAE's shares as of 31 July 2026, second after Capital Research and Management (11.47%).","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0071","ex":"BlackRock, Inc. | 9.52% | 269,568,294 | Jul 31, 2026","pg":"Top Institutional Holders table"}],"shares":269568294,"basis":"Data provider shareholder list (Investing.com); MarketScreener lists BlackRock Investment Management (UK) at 6.6%"},{"id":"OWN-0028","t":"ownership","a":"FND-00003","b":"COM-00006","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":"1.73","date":"2026-06-01","st":"reported","ctx":"State Street's asset-management arm holds a minority, mostly index-linked position in BAE Systems plc. A data provider lists it at about 1.7% of BAE's shares as of June 2026.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0071","ex":"State Street Investment Management | 1.73% | 49,052,375 | Jun 01, 2026","pg":"Top Institutional Holders table"}],"shares":49052375,"basis":"Data provider shareholder list (Investing.com); no UK holding notice required below 5%"},{"id":"OWN-0029","t":"ownership","a":"FND-00005","b":"COM-00006","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":null,"cur":"USD","pct":"11.04","date":"2026-01-22","st":"documented","ctx":"Capital Group notified BAE Systems that it held 331,390,657 voting rights, about 11.04%, after crossing a threshold on 22 January 2026.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0072","ex":"The Capital Group Companies, Inc. ... % of voting rights: 11.042616% ... 331,390,657 voting rights; threshold crossed 22-Jan-2026","pg":"TR-1 section 8/9: resulting situation"}],"shares":331390657,"basis":"TR-1 voting-rights notification, The Capital Group Companies"},{"id":"OWN-0030","t":"ownership","a":"FND-00004","b":"COM-00006","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":"5.01","date":"2024-07-29","st":"documented","ctx":"FMR LLC, Fidelity's parent, notified BAE Systems in July 2024 that it held just over 5% of the voting rights. A notice in October 2024 reporting a fall below 5% was withdrawn the next day as a processing error. Data-provider figures for June 2026 put FMR at about 4.3%.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0073","ex":"FMR LLC has notified the Company that due to a processing error, the notification it provided to the Company was incorrect and that FMR LLC's holding did not fall below the 5% threshold.","pg":"RNS announcement text"}],"shares":null,"basis":"TR-1 holding notification by FMR LLC, confirmed in BAE RNS correction of 2024-10-22"},{"id":"OWN-0031","t":"ownership","a":"FND-00006","b":"COM-00004","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":10280000000,"cur":"USD","pct":"10.02","date":null,"st":"reported","ctx":"Longview Asset Management is listed as beneficial owner of about 10% of General Dynamics (Form 3, May 2026; GD's 2026 proxy). Longview manages these shares for client accounts and reports no pecuniary interest of its own, so the money behind the stake belongs to its clients. The site values the stake at about $10.28B.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0074","ex":"This report is being filed as a result of a reduction in the number of outstanding shares of the Issuer's Common Stock as reported in the Issuer's Form 10-Q as filed on April 29, 2026.","pg":"Form 3, explanation of responses"},{"s":"SRC-V0355","ex":"Owner reports the security-ownership table lists Longview Asset Management as beneficial owner of 27.06M shares (10.0%); our fetcher received a truncated copy without that table.","pg":null}],"shares":27000000,"basis":null},{"id":"MF-0001","t":"money","a":"GOV-00001","b":"LBL-DEFENSE-SYSTEM","rel":"U.S. military spending (national defense function 050)","amt":"820000000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"FY2023","st":"reported","ctx":"The US spent about $820B on the military in FY2023, roughly 13.3% of the federal budget (USAFacts). Treasury's exact FY2023 figure is $820.7B.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0075","ex":"In 2023, the US military spent approximately $820.3 billion, or roughly 13.3% of the entire federal budget","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-0079","ex":"2023-09-30 National Defense FYTD $820,734,092,776.06; 2024-09-30 FYTD $874,040,585,085.73; 2025-09-30 FYTD $916,648,676,662.05; 2026-03-31 FYTD $481,075,854,271.40","pg":null}],"dup":["MF-0002","MF-0003","MF-0004"]},{"id":"MF-0005","t":"money","a":"GOV-00001","b":"LBL-DEFENSE-SYSTEM","rel":"U.S. military spending total","amt":"3090000000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"FY2023 to H1 FY2026 (Oct 2022 to Mar 2026)","st":"documented","ctx":"Treasury's Monthly Treasury Statements record U.S. military (function 050) outlays of $820.7 billion in FY2023, $874.0 billion in FY2024 and $916.6 billion in FY2025. In the first half of FY2026 (October 2025 to March 2026) military outlays were $481.1 billion, bringing the FY2023 to H1 FY2026 total to about $3.09 trillion.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0079","ex":"2023-09-30 National Defense FYTD $820,734,092,776.06; 2024-09-30 FYTD $874,040,585,085.73; 2025-09-30 FYTD $916,648,676,662.05; 2026-03-31 FYTD $481,075,854,271.40","pg":"MTS Table 9, classification 'National Defense', current_fytd_rcpt_outly_amt for record dates 2023-09-30, 2024-09-30, 2025-09-30, 2026-03-31 (FY2026 query: record_fiscal_year=2026)"}]},{"id":"MF-0006","t":"money","a":"GOV-00002","b":"LBL-PRIVATE-CONTRACTORS","rel":"Pentagon contracts","amt":"456000000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"FY2023","st":"documented","ctx":"The Pentagon obligated about $456B on contracts in FY2023 (GAO), more than all other federal agencies combined. This money flows to private arms makers.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0080","ex":"In fiscal year 2023, the Department of Defense (DOD) obligated about $456 billion on contracts","pg":"p. 1"}],"dup":["MF-0007","MF-0008","MF-0009"]},{"id":"MF-0010","t":"money","a":"GOV-00002","b":"LBL-PRIVATE-CONTRACTORS","rel":"Pentagon contracts total","amt":"1762800000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"FY2023 to FY2026 to date","st":"documented","ctx":"USAspending.gov reports Department of Defense contract obligations of $456.9 billion in FY2023, $445.8 billion in FY2024 and $491.8 billion in FY2025, about $1.39 trillion over three fiscal years. FY2026 data published so far adds $368.3 billion, for about $1.76 trillion through the latest available FY2026 data.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0084","ex":"FY2025: {\"total_aggregated_amount\":501674746951.4,\"results\":[{\"category\":\"contracts\",\"aggregated_amount\":491788672185.41}... FY2024 contracts 445756409898.65; FY2023 contracts 456947663232.64","pg":"obligations_by_award_category, agency 097, fiscal_year=2023/2024/2025/2026, category 'contracts'"}]},{"id":"MF-0011","t":"money","a":"GOV-00001","b":"GOV-00005","rel":"U.S. military assistance to Israel","amt":"21700000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"Oct 2023 to Sep 2025","st":"reported","ctx":"Brown University's Costs of War Project estimates that the U.S. spent $21.7 billion on military aid to Israel from October 2023 to September 2025, not counting arms sales agreed for later delivery. Under the 2016 memorandum of understanding, the U.S. also provides a baseline of $3.8 billion a year.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0085","ex":"$21.7 billion on military aid to Israel ... does not include the tens of billions of dollars in arms sales agreements that have been committed for weapons and services that will be paid for and delivered in the years to come.","pg":"Summary"}]},{"id":"MF-0012","t":"money","a":"GOV-00001","b":"LBL-REGIONAL-MILITARY-OPERATIONS","rel":"U.S. regional military operations","amt":"12070000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"Oct 2023 to Sep 2025","st":"reported","ctx":"Brown University's Costs of War Project estimates the cost of U.S. military operations in the wider Middle East (including Yemen and the Red Sea) from October 2023 to September 2025 at $9.65 to $12.07 billion. The 2026 the war on Iran is recorded separately.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0086","ex":"Economist Linda J. Bilmes (Senior Lecturer, Harvard University) estimates the costs of these actions through September 2025 to range from U.S. $9.65 - $12.07 billion.","pg":"Summary"}]},{"id":"MF-0013","t":"money","a":"GOV-00001","b":"GOV-00005","rel":"Historical U.S. support to Israel","amt":"300000000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"since 1948","st":"reported","ctx":"Israel is the largest cumulative recipient of US foreign aid, with over $300B in economic and military assistance since 1948, adjusted for inflation (Council on Foreign Relations).","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0087","ex":"Israel has been the largest cumulative recipient of U.S. foreign aid since its founding, receiving over $300 billion (adjusted for inflation) in total economic and military assistance.","pg":null}]},{"id":"MF-0014","t":"money","a":"LBL-GLOBAL-CUSTOMERS","b":"LBL-ISRAELI-DEFENSE-INDUSTRY","rel":"Israeli arms export agreements","amt":"13073000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2023","st":"documented","ctx":"Israel signed a record $13.07B in new arms export deals in 2023, the third record year in a row, according to Israel's Ministry of Defense. It rose to over $14.7B in 2024.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0088","ex":"Israel again reached an all-time peak in defense exports in 2023 ... total $13.073 billion","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-0089","ex":"over $14.7 billion in 2024 – a 13% increase over the previous year ... significant growth in contracts with European countries, which accounted for 54% of the total deal volume in 2024 ... Missile, rocket, and air defense systems (48%)","pg":null}],"dup":["MF-0015","MF-0016"]},{"id":"MF-0017","t":"money","a":"LBL-EUROPE","b":"LBL-ISRAELI-DEFENSE-INDUSTRY","rel":"European share of Israeli arms export agreements (approx.)","amt":"19477000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2023–2025","st":"documented","ctx":"Europe made up 35% of Israel's arms export deals in 2023 and 54% in 2024, according to Israel's Ministry of Defense. The $19.48B 2023–2025 figure is an estimate (about 41.4% of $47.07B) derived from rounded regional shares.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0089","ex":"Europe: 54% of 2024 deals (compared to 35% in 2023)","pg":"Geographic distribution"}]},{"id":"MF-0018","t":"money","a":"GOV-00003","b":"LBL-ISRAEL-SECURITY-SUPPLEMENTAL-APPROPRIATI","rel":"U.S. Israel security supplemental appropriation","amt":"26380000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"April 2024","st":"documented","ctx":"The Israel Security Supplemental Appropriations Act, enacted in April 2024, totaled $26.38 billion. It included $4 billion for Iron Dome and David's Sling, $1.2 billion for Iron Beam, $3.5 billion in Foreign Military Financing, $4.4 billion to replenish U.S. stocks provided to Israel and $2.4 billion for U.S. operations in the region.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0091","ex":"The bill totals $26.38 billion to support Israel in its effort to defend ... $4 billion to replenish Iron Dome and David's Sling missile ... $1.2 billion for the Iron Beam defense system ... $3.5 billion for the procurement of advanced weapons systems ... $4.4 billion to replenish defense articles and defense services ... $2.4 billion for current U.S. military operations in the region","pg":null}]},{"id":"MF-0019","t":"money","a":"GOV-00002","b":"LBL-U-S-MILITARY-OPERATIONS-AGAINST-IRAN","rel":"U.S. war cost: Iran war (Operation Epic Fury)","amt":"38000000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-02-28 to 2026-08-01","st":"documented","ctx":"The United States began combat operations against Iran (Operation Epic Fury) on February 28, 2026. CBO estimated the war had cost the Department of Defense about $38 billion as of August 1, 2026; Bloomberg Government reported a Pentagon estimate to Congress of $43.6 billion as of September 3, 2026.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0092","ex":"CBO estimates that as of August 1, 2026, the armed conflict with Iran has cost DoD approximately $38 billion. ... The United States commenced Operation Epic Fury on February 28, 2026, with air and cruise missile strikes against Iran","pg":"p. 2 (cost estimate); p. 5 (start date)"}]},{"id":"BEN-0001","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00004","b":"PER-00013","rel":"Executive compensation","amt":"72302000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2023–2025","st":"documented","ctx":"Phebe N. Novakovic received $72.3M in total reported compensation as General Dynamics CEO over 2023–2025 ($22.6M, $23.8M, $25.9M) while the company supplied bomb bodies to Israel.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0115","ex":"Summary Compensation Table Total for PEO: 2025 $25,924,082; 2024 $23,794,702; 2023 $22,582,776","pg":"Pay Versus Performance table, Summary Compensation Table Total for PEO"}]},{"id":"BEN-0002","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00002","b":"PER-00012","rel":"Executive compensation","amt":"70021000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2023–2025","st":"documented","ctx":"James D. Taiclet received $70.0M in total reported compensation as Lockheed Martin CEO over 2023–2025, years in which Lockheed's F-35s and Hellfires were used by Israel in Gaza.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0116","ex":"PEO Total Compensation Amount: 2025 $23,453,308; 2024 $23,753,914; 2023 $22,813,775","pg":"Pay Versus Performance table, PEO Total Compensation Amount"}]},{"id":"BEN-0003","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00001","b":"PER-00014","rel":"Executive compensation","amt":"55595000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2023–2025","st":"documented","ctx":"Christopher T. Calio received $55.6M in total reported compensation from RTX over 2023–2025 ($12.7M, $18.0M, $24.8M).","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0117","ex":"Christopher T. Calio: 2025 $24,846,826; 2024 $18,004,831. [2024 proxy SCT] Calio 2023 Total $12,743,432","pg":"Pay Versus Performance table (2025, 2024); 2024 proxy Summary Compensation Table (2023)"}]},{"id":"BEN-0004","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00003","b":"PER-00015","rel":"Executive compensation","amt":"23581000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2025","st":"documented","ctx":"Robert K. Ortberg received $23.6M in total reported compensation as Boeing CEO in 2025, his first full year, while Boeing supplied JDAM kits and GBU-39 bombs to Israel.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0118","ex":"Robert K. Ortberg (PEO): 2025: $23,581,389; 2024: $18,388,629","pg":"Pay Versus Performance table, SCT Total for PEO"}]},{"id":"BEN-0006","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00007","b":"COM-00035","rel":"Market value of controlling stake","amt":"13588000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-09-29","st":"documented","ctx":"This is the market value of Federmann Enterprises' controlling block of 19,318,508 Elbit shares (about 41.2% of the vote) as of 29 September 2026. It implies about $703 per share. The figure is a mark-to-market paper value, not realised profit.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0009","ex":"As of June 17, 2026, Federmann Enterprises Ltd. held 19,318,508 shares ... Mr. Federmann controls, directly and indirectly, the vote of Shares owned by Heris and FEL (approximately 41.2% of our outstanding Shares).","pg":"Security ownership of principal shareholders"}]},{"id":"BEN-0007","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00007","b":"FAM-N0001","rel":"Family look-through stake value","amt":"8400000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-09-29","st":"documented","ctx":"Michael Federmann and his sons David, Gideon and Daniel together hold an indirect economic (look-through) interest of about 25.5% of Elbit's 46.8M shares, worth about $8.4B at the 29 September 2026 market value. This is a paper value, not cash received.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0009","ex":"Michael Federmann and his sons (David, Gideon, and Daniel) collectively hold an indirect economic interest of approximately 25.5% of outstanding shares. ","pg":"Security ownership of principal shareholders"}]},{"id":"BEN-0009","t":"benefit","a":"COM-00020","b":"PER-00036","rel":"Change-in-control compensation","amt":"16272626","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2024","st":"reported","ctx":"Estimated 'golden parachute' payments of $16.27M in Kaman's March 2024 merger proxy for its named executive officers, Ian K. Walsh, chairman, president and CEO, among them, triggered by Arcline's $1.8B takeover that closed in April 2024. Kaman made bomb fuzes for US munitions.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0119","ex":"EDGAR full-text search for \"16,272,626\" returns KAMAN Corp DEFM14A filed 2024-03-08 (accession 0001140361-24-012403, ny20021849x2_defm14a.htm).","pg":"Golden Parachute Compensation table (Interests of Kaman's Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger)"},{"s":"SRC-0041","ex":"completion of its acquisition by Arcline Investment Management, L.P. (\"Arcline\") in an all-cash transaction with a total enterprise value of approximately $1.8 billion.","pg":null}]},{"id":"ELC-0001","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00059","rel":"spent_supporting","amt":"1700000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"MD-04 House primary · 2022 · Won / advanced per research","st":"reported","ctx":"The United Democracy Project, AIPAC's super PAC, spent about $1.7 million supporting Glenn Ivey and about $4.26 million opposing Donna Edwards in the 2022 Democratic primary for Maryland's 4th District. Ivey won with 51% to Edwards' 35%.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"UDP spent $4.26 million against Edwards and $1.7 million for the winning candidate, Glenn Ivey","pg":"Maryland 4th District paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0002","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00060","rel":"spent_supporting","amt":"3900000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"MI-11 House primary · 2022 · Won / advanced per research","st":"reported","ctx":"In the 2022 Democratic primary for Michigan's 11th District, which put two incumbents against each other after redistricting, UDP spent about $3.88 million supporting Haley Stevens and about $342,000 opposing Andy Levin. Stevens won the primary.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"In the state's 11th Congressional District, UDP spent $3.88 million supporting Haley Stevens and $342,000 against Andy Levin ... Stevens won the primary","pg":"Michigan districts paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0003","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00061","rel":"spent_supporting","amt":"2740000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"MI-13 House primary · 2022 · Lost to Shri Thanedar","st":"reported","ctx":"In the 2022 Democratic primary for Michigan's 13th District, UDP spent about $2.74 million supporting state Sen. Adam Hollier and about $1.43 million opposing Shri Thanedar. Thanedar won the primary.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"in the 13th Congressional District, UDP spent $2.74 million in support of Adam Hollier and $1.43 million opposing Shri Thanedar. Stevens won the primary, but Hollier lost his race to Thanedar.","pg":"Michigan districts paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0004","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00062","rel":"spent_supporting","amt":"660000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"PA-12 House primary · 2022 · Lost to Summer Lee","st":"reported","ctx":"In the May 2022 Democratic primary for Pennsylvania's 12th District, UDP spent about $660,000 supporting Steve Irwin and about $2 million opposing Summer Lee, close to $2.7 million in total. Lee narrowly won.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"In Pennsylvania's 12th District, UDP's preferred candidate, Steve Irwin, lost the May 17 Democratic primary. The group had spent $2 million against Summer Lee, who won, and $660,000 for Irwin.","pg":"Pennsylvania 12th District paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0005","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00063","rel":"spent_opposing","amt":"2300000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"NJ-11 special Democratic primary (2026-02-05) · 2026 · Lost to Analilia Mejia","st":"reported","ctx":"In the February 2026 special Democratic primary for New Jersey's 11th District, UDP spent about $2.3 million on ads opposing former Rep. Tom Malinowski. Analilia Mejia won by fewer than 1,000 votes, and Malinowski conceded on 2026-02-10.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0130","ex":"AIPAC pummeled Malinowski with $2.3 million in negative ads. ... Mejia ... was ahead of Malinowski by less than 1,000 votes when he conceded the race.","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0080","ex":"more than $1 million supporting Adrian Boafo, who won the Democratic primary ... over $3 million opposing Rep. Thomas Massie in the Republican primary ... The super PAC also spent nearly $500,000 supporting Gallrein. ... spent over $2 million opposing Tom Malinowski, who lost a special Democratic primary [FEC data through June 30, 2026]","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0081","ex":"Independent Expenditures Total: $53,414,686.72 ... Total Receipts: $109,721,116.19 ... Ending Cash on Hand: $54,918,846.22 ... Coverage End Date: August 31, 2026","pg":null}],"ev (add source) + ctx note":"FactCheck.org's FEC-based review (through 30 June 2026) found over $2 million opposing Tom Malinowski; FEC filings are canonical and the total may have grown since."},{"id":"ELC-0006","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00065","rel":"spent_opposing","amt":"4200000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"MD-04 House primary · 2022 · Lost primary per research","st":"reported","ctx":"Opposing Donna Edwards in the 2022 Maryland 4th District Democratic primary was UDP's largest single target that cycle, at about $4.27 million in FEC-reported independent expenditures. Edwards lost to Glenn Ivey.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0131","ex":"This committee is active in congressional races and has spent most of its money, $4,268,441, opposing Donna Edwards.","pg":"Committee summary"}]},{"id":"ELC-0007","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00066","rel":"spent_opposing","amt":"340000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"MI-11 House primary · 2022 · Lost primary per research","st":"reported","ctx":"UDP spent about $342,000 opposing Rep. Andy Levin in the 2022 Michigan 11th District Democratic primary, alongside about $3.88 million supporting Haley Stevens. Levin lost.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"In the state's 11th Congressional District, UDP spent $3.88 million supporting Haley Stevens and $342,000 against Andy Levin ... Stevens won the primary","pg":"Michigan districts paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0008","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00067","rel":"spent_opposing","amt":"2000000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"PA-12 House primary · 2022 · Won","st":"reported","ctx":"UDP spent about $2 million opposing state Rep. Summer Lee in the May 2022 Democratic primary for Pennsylvania's 12th District, part of nearly $2.7 million it spent in the race. Lee narrowly defeated Steve Irwin.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"The group had spent $2 million against Summer Lee, who won, and $660,000 for Irwin.","pg":"Pennsylvania 12th District paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0009","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00068","rel":"spent_opposing","amt":"1400000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"MI-13 House primary · 2022 · Won primary per research","st":"reported","ctx":"UDP spent about $1.43 million opposing Shri Thanedar in the August 2022 Michigan 13th District Democratic primary, while backing Adam Hollier. Thanedar won the primary.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0129","ex":"UDP spent $2.74 million in support of Adam Hollier and $1.43 million opposing Shri Thanedar. Stevens won the primary, but Hollier lost his race to Thanedar.","pg":"Michigan districts paragraph"}]},{"id":"ELC-0010","t":"election","a":"ORG-00003","b":"PER-00069","rel":"spent_opposing","amt":"4600000","cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"CA-47 House primary · 2024 · Won primary / advanced per research","st":"reported","ctx":"UDP spent about $4.6 to $4.7 million opposing state Sen. Dave Min in California's March 2024 top-two primary for the 47th District. Min finished in the top two and advanced to face Republican Scott Baugh.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0132","ex":"United Democracy Project also spent about $4.6 million trying to defeat California state Sen. Dave Min in the primary for the state's 47th Congressional District. ... Min ... finished in the top two, and will face Republican Scott Baugh","pg":null}]},{"id":"CON-0001","t":"consumer","a":"PER-00004","b":"COM-00019","rel":"Founder/chair; economic interest","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Larry Ellison founded Oracle in 1977, was CEO until 2014 and remains chairman and a major shareholder, so Oracle's government and military business adds to his personal wealth.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0142","ex":"He founded the company in 1977 and served as CEO until September 2014.","pg":null}]},{"id":"CON-0006","t":"consumer","a":"PER-00024","b":"COM-00011","rel":"Founder/shareholder","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Jeff Bezos founded Amazon in 1994 and remains its chair and a major shareholder. AWS's Project Nimbus contract with the Israeli government is corporate business that adds to his wealth.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-0147","ex":"Jeff Bezos founded Amazon.com in 1994.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0001","t":"contract","a":"COM-00003","b":"CTR-2026-APACHE","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":null,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-01-30","st":"documented","ctx":"On 30 January 2026 the US notified Congress of a $3.8B sale to Israel of 30 AH-64E Apache attack helicopters, naming Boeing (Arlington, VA) and Lockheed Martin (Orlando, FL) as principal contractors. It was part of a $6.67B set of Israel sales notified that day, which also included $1.98B of JLTVs and $740M of Namer power packs.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0001","ex":"thirty (30) AH-64E Apache attack helicopters ... $3.8 billion ... The Boeing Company, located in Arlington, VA; and Lockheed Martin, located in Orlando, FL","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0331","ex":"January 30 ... $6.67 billion ($3.8B Apache; $1.98B JLTV; $740M power packs; $150M helicopters) ... March 6 ... $151.8 million ... Emergency declaration ... May 1 ... $992.4 million ... Emergency declaration","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0002","t":"contract","a":"COM-00002","b":"CTR-2026-APACHE","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":null,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-01-30","st":"documented","ctx":"Lockheed Martin (Orlando, FL) is a named principal contractor, with Boeing, on the $3.8B sale of 30 AH-64E Apache helicopters to Israel notified on 30 January 2026.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0001","ex":"thirty (30) AH-64E Apache attack helicopters ... $3.8 billion ... The Boeing Company, located in Arlington, VA; and Lockheed Martin, located in Orlando, FL","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0003","t":"contract","a":"COM-00006","b":"CTR-2026-APKWS","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":null,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-05-01","st":"documented","ctx":"On 1 May 2026, during Israel's war on Iran, the State Department used emergency powers to skip congressional review of a $992.4M sale to Israel of 10,000 APKWS-II laser-guided rockets. BAE Systems (Nashua, NH) is the principal contractor.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0332","ex":"ten thousand (10,000) Advanced Precision Kill Weapon System-II All Up Round ... $992.4 million ... BAE Systems, located in Nashua, NH ... The Secretary of State has determined and provided detailed justification that an emergency exists","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0331","ex":"January 30 ... $6.67 billion ($3.8B Apache; $1.98B JLTV; $740M power packs; $150M helicopters) ... March 6 ... $151.8 million ... Emergency declaration ... May 1 ... $992.4 million ... Emergency declaration","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0004","t":"contract","a":"COM-00022","b":"CTR-2026-BLU110","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":null,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-03-06","st":"documented","ctx":"In March 2026 the State Department waived congressional review on emergency grounds for a $151.8M sale to Israel of 12,000 BLU-110A/B 1,000-pound bomb bodies. Repkon USA, which runs the former General Dynamics Garland bomb-body plant, is the principal contractor; it renamed itself Paligen Technologies days later after a backlash. Emergency sales that month also included $298M for 5,000 small-diameter bombs and $209M for 10,000 500-pound bombs.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0002","ex":"12,000 BLU-110A/B general purpose 1,000-pound bomb bodies ... estimated value of $151.8m ... Repkon USA, a subsidiary of Türkiye based Repkon, has been selected as the principal contractor ... waives the Congressional review requirements under Section 36(b)","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0333","ex":"In April 2025, Repkon USA acquired \"General Dynamics Ordnance and Tactical Systems' Garland operations in Texas ... \" ... new name ... Paligen Technologies","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0334","ex":"$298 million for 5,000 ... small-diameter bombs; $209 million for 10,000 500-pound bombs; $151.8 million for 12,000 1,000-pound bombs","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0003","ex":"Israel will also receive 10,000 bombs weighing roughly 250 kilograms, 5,000 small-diameter bombs ... the only facility in the United States capable of producing bomb bodies for the MK-80 series","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0005","t":"contract","a":"COM-00004","b":"CTR-2026-MK84","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":null,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-09-15","st":"reported","ctx":"In September 2026 the administration informally notified Congress of a $2.8B FMF-funded package for Israel of 20,000 MK-84 and 20,000 BLU-117 2,000-pound bombs plus I-2000 penetrator warheads, which the Washington Post and AFP called the largest single sale of the munition in years. Rep. Gregory Meeks and Sen. Jeanne Shaheen placed holds on it. No contractor has been officially named; the link to General Dynamics is an inference from the 2025 case and is reported only.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0319","ex":"40,000 bombs: 20,000 MK-84 general-purpose bombs and 20,000 BLU-117s ... along with I-2000 Penetrator warheads ... The deal has been informally notified to congressional committees.","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0335","ex":"Rep. Gregory Meeks ... would \"not clear this sale at this time\" ... Sen. Jeanne Shaheen ... \"placed a hold on the weapons sale\" ... the sale remains halted at the moment","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0020","t":"ownership","a":"COM-00013","b":"COM-00015","rel":"owns","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":100,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Google Cloud is a division of Google, owned by Alphabet. Its revenue is reported as an Alphabet segment ($24.77B in Q2 2026).","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0126","ex":"Q2 2026 revenue: $119.8 billion ... Google Cloud revenue: $24.77 billion (compared to $13.62 billion a year prior) [fetch-tool rendering]","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0014","ex":"Google has officially acquired Israeli cybersecurity firm Wiz for $32 billion in cash ... the company will join Google Cloud","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0021","t":"ownership","a":"COM-00011","b":"COM-00012","rel":"owns","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":100,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"AWS is Amazon's cloud division and is reported as an Amazon segment ($42.2B in Q2 2026).","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0121","ex":"$200.6 billion in the second quarter ... AWS segment sales increased 37% year-over-year to $42.2 billion","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0022","t":"ownership","a":"COM-00016","b":"COM-00017","rel":"shareholder_of","amt":135000000000,"cur":"USD","pct":27,"date":"2025-10-28","st":"documented","ctx":"After OpenAI's restructuring into a public benefit corporation, Microsoft holds about 27% of OpenAI Group PBC, worth about $135B.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0130","ex":"Microsoft will still hold a stake of about $135bn, or 27 percent, in OpenAI Group PBC","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0023","t":"edge","a":"COM-00011","b":"COM-00018","rel":"investor_in","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":"2026-06-30","st":"documented","ctx":"Amazon booked a $53.4B pre-tax gain in Q2 2026, mainly from its investments in Anthropic.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0122","ex":"Second quarter 2026 net income includes non-operating pre-tax other income of $53.4 billion, primarily from our investments in Anthropic.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0024","t":"edge","a":"COM-00016","b":"COM-00018","rel":"investor_in","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":"2026-06-30","st":"documented","ctx":"Microsoft reported a $3.2B gain on its Anthropic investment in its fiscal Q4 2026.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0129","ex":"Annual revenue totaled $331.8 billion ... Net income came in at $133.7 billion [fetch-tool rendering] ... a $3.2 billion gain from the company's investment in Anthropic","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0025","t":"edge","a":"COM-00010","b":"COM-00018","rel":"partnership_with","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":"2024-11-07","st":"documented","ctx":"Palantir and Anthropic, with AWS, put Claude on classified networks for U.S. intelligence and military agencies. According to the Washington Post, Claude was later central to Palantir's Maven targeting system in the Iran war.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0124","ex":"Anthropic and Palantir Technologies Inc. (NYSE: PLTR) today announced a partnership with Amazon Web Services (AWS) to provide U.S. intelligence and defense agencies access to the Claude 3 and 3.5 family of models on AWS.","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0115","ex":"Claude is central to Palantir's Maven Smart System, which provides real-time targeting for military operations against Iran. ... proposed 'hundreds' of targets for the U.S. military to strike, prioritized them in order of importance, and provided location coordinates for them.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0026","t":"edge","a":"COM-00012","b":"COM-00018","rel":"hosts_models_for","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":"2024-11-07","st":"documented","ctx":"AWS hosts Anthropic's Claude for U.S. intelligence and military agencies under the Palantir partnership.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0124","ex":"Anthropic and Palantir Technologies Inc. (NYSE: PLTR) today announced a partnership with Amazon Web Services (AWS) to provide U.S. intelligence and defense agencies access to the Claude 3 and 3.5 family of models on AWS.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0027","t":"edge","a":"COM-00003","b":"GOV-00007","rel":"supplies_aircraft_to","amt":8600000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-01-02","st":"documented","ctx":"The U.S. Air Force awarded Boeing $8.6B for 25 new F-15IA jets for the Israeli Air Force, with an option for 25 more.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0101","ex":"an $8.6 billion contract to The Boeing Co. ... 25 new F-15IA aircraft for the Israeli Air Force with an option for an additional 25","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0028","t":"edge","a":"COM-00003","b":"GOV-00007","rel":"supplies_munitions_to","amt":289000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-03","st":"reported","ctx":"Boeing reportedly agreed a deal worth up to $289M to supply Israel with up to 5,000 smart bombs.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0102","ex":"a deal worth up to $289 million to supply Israel with as many as 5,000 smart bombs","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0356","ex":"Boeing signs $289 million Israel contract for 5,000 smart bombs, Bloomberg News reports [headline from URL; body blocked to our fetcher]","pg":null}],"ev (add source)":"https://www.reuters.com/world/middle-east/boeing-signs-289-million-israel-contract-5000-smart-bombs-bloomberg-news-reports-2026-03-10/"},{"id":"REL-V0029","t":"edge","a":"COM-00003","b":"WPN-00010","rel":"manufactures_components_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Boeing produces 40–50% of Arrow 3 content, including motorcases, canisters and guidance parts, with IAI as prime contractor.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0103","ex":"The attack was intercepted and thwarted by missile interception systems, such as the Israeli Arrow 3 system [April 2024]; jointly produced by IAI and Boeing, Boeing responsible for 40–50 percent of production content","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0030","t":"edge","a":"COM-00003","b":"CTR-2026-APACHE","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":3800000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-01-30","st":"documented","ctx":"Notified to Congress Jan 30, 2026: 30 AH-64E Apaches for Israel, estimated at $3.8B.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0001","ex":"thirty (30) AH-64E Apache attack helicopters ... $3.8 billion ... The Boeing Company, located in Arlington, VA; and Lockheed Martin, located in Orlando, FL","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0031","t":"edge","a":"COM-00002","b":"CTR-2026-APACHE","rel":"principal_contractor_for","amt":3800000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-01-30","st":"documented","ctx":"Lockheed Martin (Orlando) is named a principal contractor on the Apache package.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0001","ex":"thirty (30) AH-64E Apache attack helicopters ... $3.8 billion ... The Boeing Company, located in Arlington, VA; and Lockheed Martin, located in Orlando, FL","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0032","t":"edge","a":"COM-00002","b":"GOV-00006","rel":"sells_aircraft_to","amt":3000000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2024-06-04","st":"documented","ctx":"Israel signed for 25 more F-35s, about $3B financed by U.S. military aid, with deliveries from 2028. That brings its fleet to 75.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0096","ex":"The deal totals some $3 billion, financed by US military aid to Israel ... 25 advanced stealth fighters built by Lockheed Martin","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0033","t":"edge","a":"COM-00005","b":"WPN-NEW-F35","rel":"manufactures_components_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Northrop Grumman builds the F-35 center fuselage and delivered it for Israel's first F-35.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0105","ex":"Northrop Grumman Delivers Center Fuselage for First Israeli F-35 Aircraft [headline]","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0034","t":"edge","a":"COM-00006","b":"WPN-NEW-F35","rel":"manufactures_components_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":13,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"BAE holds a 13–15% workshare of each F-35 and builds every rear fuselage.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0107","ex":"we hold a 13-15% workshare of each aircraft ... The rear fuselage of every F-35 in the global fleet is built at our advanced manufacturing and assembly facilities in the UK.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0035","t":"edge","a":"COM-00002","b":"WPN-NEW-F35","rel":"manufactures_or_supplies","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Lockheed Martin is prime contractor of the F-35. The UN Special Rapporteur says F-35s and F-16s were integral to Israel dropping about 85,000 tons of bombs on Gaza.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0004","ex":"Israel benefits from the largest-ever defence procurement programme – for the F-35 fighter jet, led by United States-based Lockheed Martin, alongside at least 1,650 other companies.","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0004","ex":"F-35s and F-16s have been integral to equipping Israel with the unprecedented aerial power to drop an estimated 85,000 tons of bombs","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0036","t":"edge","a":"COM-00001","b":"WPN-NEW-F35","rel":"manufactures_components_of","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"RTX's Pratt & Whitney builds the F-35's engines.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0095","ex":"Raytheon ... manufactures the aircraft's engines through its subsidiary Pratt & Whitney [F-35]; Raytheon's 5,000-pound GBU-28 'bunker buster' and laser-guided Paveway bombs","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0037","t":"edge","a":"COM-00001","b":"WPN-NEW-TOMAHAWK","rel":"manufactures_or_supplies","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"Raytheon makes the Tomahawk cruise missile.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0006","ex":"The Tomahawk cruise missile is a precision weapon that launches from ships, submarines, and ground launchers and can strike targets precisely from 1,000 miles away","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0038","t":"edge","a":"WPN-NEW-TOMAHAWK","b":"LBL-U-S-MILITARY-OPERATIONS-AGAINST-IRAN","rel":"used_in","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":"2026-02-28","st":"documented","ctx":"A U.S. Tomahawk struck the Shajareh Tayyebeh elementary school in Minab, Iran, killing 156 to 168 people, most of them children. A preliminary U.S. probe cited outdated intelligence.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0007","ex":"On 28 February 2026, the first day of the 2026 Iran war, the United States Armed Forces struck the Shajareh Tayyebeh Elementary School in Minab, Hormozgan province in southern Iran with a Tomahawk missile, directly killing 156 civilians, including 120 schoolchildren","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0008","ex":"A preliminary US military investigation has found that American forces were responsible for the devastating February 28 Tomahawk missile strike that tore through an elementary school ... officers at US Central Command generated strike coordinates using outdated intelligence","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0094","ex":"At least 168 people were killed — more than 100 of them under the age of 12. ... Was artificial intelligence, including the use of the Maven Smart System, used to identify the Shajareh Tayyebeh school as a target?","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0039","t":"edge","a":"COM-00001","b":"COM-NEW-R2S","rel":"joint_venture_partner_in","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"R2S is the Raytheon–Rafael joint venture that builds Tamir interceptors for Iron Dome.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0009","ex":"The Raytheon-Rafael Protection Systems (R2S) joint venture was awarded a $1.25 billion contract to supply Israel with Tamir surface-to-air missiles.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0040","t":"edge","a":"COM-00009","b":"COM-NEW-R2S","rel":"joint_venture_partner_in","amt":null,"cur":null,"pct":null,"date":null,"st":"documented","ctx":"R2S is the Raytheon–Rafael joint venture that builds Tamir interceptors for Iron Dome.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0009","ex":"The Raytheon-Rafael Protection Systems (R2S) joint venture was awarded a $1.25 billion contract to supply Israel with Tamir surface-to-air missiles.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0041","t":"edge","a":"COM-NEW-R2S","b":"GOV-00006","rel":"contractor_for","amt":1250000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2025-11-21","st":"documented","ctx":"A $1.25B contract from the Israel Missile Defense Organization for Tamir interceptors, to be built in Camden, Arkansas.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0009","ex":"The Raytheon-Rafael Protection Systems (R2S) joint venture was awarded a $1.25 billion contract to supply Israel with Tamir surface-to-air missiles.","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0042","t":"edge","a":"COM-00022","b":"CTR-NEW-BLU110-2026","rel":"prime_contractor_for","amt":151800000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-03-07","st":"documented","ctx":"An emergency sale bypassing Congress: 12,000 BLU-110 1,000-lb bomb bodies for Israel during the Iran war.","ev":[{"s":"SRC-V0002","ex":"12,000 BLU-110A/B general purpose 1,000-pound bomb bodies ... $151.8m ... Repkon USA, a subsidiary of Türkiye based Repkon","pg":null},{"s":"SRC-V0003","ex":"the prime contractor for the project is Repkon USA, based in Garland, Texas ... Repkon USA is a subsidiary of the Turkish arms manufacturer Repkon ... Secretary of State Marco Rubio declared that the war with Iran constitutes an 'emergency requiring immediate sale'","pg":null}]},{"id":"REL-V0043","t":"edge","a":"COM-00007","b":"GOV-00006","rel":"contractor_for","amt":431000000,"cur":"USD","pct":null,"date":"2026-01 to 2026-04","st":"documented","ctx":"Israel MoD contracts with Elbit in 2026: $183M for air munitions (Jan), about $200M for air-launched munitions tied to the Iran campaign 